window.POP_REGISTER_CONTENT("2peter", {"intro":{"type":"masterIntroduction","book":"2 Peter","chapters":"3","bigPicture":{"subtitle":"Grow, Remember, and Wait for the Day","paragraphs":["Picture an old fisherman in a prison or a rented room in Rome, knowing that his execution is near. Decades earlier Jesus had told him by the Sea of Galilee that when he was old, someone else would dress him and carry him where he did not want to go. That day has almost come. He has one more letter to write, and he writes it as a man packing up his tent: “knowing that the putting off of my tent comes swiftly, even as our Lord Jesus Christ made clear to me” (2 Peter 1:14).","His first letter dealt with pressure from outside — persecution, slander, a hostile world. This letter deals with danger from inside. False teachers have arrived in the churches. They are clever, persuasive and greedy; they promise freedom while living in moral corruption; and they mock the promise of Christ's return. “Where is the promise of his coming?” they ask (2 Peter 3:4). Peter's last words are a defence of the truth and a call to grow.","The letter has three short chapters. Chapter 1 shows what God has given — “all things that pertain to life and godliness” (2 Peter 1:3) — and calls believers to add virtue upon virtue to their faith, resting on the sure word of eyewitnesses and prophets. Chapter 2 exposes the false teachers with the fierce language of the Old Testament prophets, and promises that God knows how to rescue the godly and judge the ungodly. Chapter 3 answers the scoffers: God's apparent delay is patience, the day of the Lord will come like a thief, and believers look for new heavens and a new earth.","The letter names its author as “Simon Peter, a servant and apostle of Jesus Christ” (2 Peter 1:1), an eyewitness of the transfiguration who wrote an earlier letter (3:1). Many modern scholars doubt that Peter wrote it, because its Greek style differs from 1 Peter and it was accepted slowly by the early church. But the letter's own claims are direct and personal; a different helper may explain the difference in style (1 Peter was written through Silvanus); and the church received it as Scripture. This study reads it as it presents itself: the last testament of the apostle Peter."]},"bridge":{"fromBook":"1 Peter","intro":"1 Peter ended by pointing ahead to 2 Peter and 1 John. Peter's two letters belong together: the first strengthens believers to suffer well; the second protects them from being deceived.","points":[{"title":"From Pressure Outside to Danger Inside","body":"1 Peter prepared the church for persecution: “Beloved, don’t be astonished at the fiery trial which has come upon you” (1 Peter 4:12). 2 Peter prepares it for deception: “But false prophets also arose among the people, as false teachers will also be among you, who will secretly bring in destructive heresies” (2 Peter 2:1). The enemy who roared like a lion in 1 Peter 5:8 now whispers like a teacher. Both are dangerous; the second is harder to see."},{"title":"From ‘Born Again’ to ‘Partakers of the Divine Nature’","body":"1 Peter celebrated new birth: God “caused us to be born again to a living hope” (1 Peter 1:3). 2 Peter shows where new birth leads: through God's promises believers “may become partakers of the divine nature, having escaped from the corruption that is in the world by lust” (2 Peter 1:4). And it calls them to grow: “But grow in the grace and knowledge of our Lord and Savior Jesus Christ” (2 Peter 3:18). The newborn who craved milk must now mature."},{"title":"From ‘A Witness of the Sufferings’ to ‘Eyewitnesses of His Majesty’","body":"In 1 Peter, Peter called himself “a witness of the sufferings of Christ” (1 Peter 5:1). In 2 Peter he remembers the mountain where he saw Christ's glory: “For we did not follow cunningly devised fables, when we made known to you the power and coming of our Lord Jesus Christ, but we were eyewitnesses of his majesty” (2 Peter 1:16). The man who saw both the cross and the transfiguration bears witness that the sufferings and the glory are both real."},{"title":"From Noah's Ark to the Coming Fire","body":"1 Peter spoke of Noah's family brought safely through the water (1 Peter 3:20). 2 Peter returns to Noah twice: God “preserved Noah with seven others, a preacher of righteousness, when he brought a flood on the world of the ungodly” (2 Peter 2:5), and the scoffers forget that the world once perished by water (3:6). The flood is a warning that God's patience has an end, and a promise that He knows how to rescue the godly."}]},"coreTheme":{"title":"Grow in Grace and Knowledge While You Wait for the Day","anchorRef":"2 Peter 1:3-11; 3:9-18","paragraphs":["The letter begins and ends with knowledge and growth. It opens with “Grace to you and peace be multiplied in the knowledge of God and of Jesus our Lord” (2 Peter 1:2), and it closes with “But grow in the grace and knowledge of our Lord and Savior Jesus Christ” (2 Peter 3:18). Between them stands a ladder of virtues: faith, moral excellence, knowledge, self-control, perseverance, godliness, brotherly affection and love (1:5–7). Knowing Christ is not a single moment but a lifetime of growing.","That growth is the answer to false teaching. Those who grow will not be swept away: “For if you do these things, you will never stumble” (2 Peter 1:10). Those who stop growing become ‘blind’ and forget their cleansing (1:9), and they are easy prey for teachers who twist the Scriptures. Peter's final warning is the same: “You therefore, beloved, knowing these things beforehand, beware, lest being carried away with the error of the wicked, you fall from your own steadfastness” (2 Peter 3:17).","And growth happens in the light of the end. The scoffers say nothing changes. Peter answers that God's timing is not ours — “one day is with the Lord as a thousand years, and a thousand years as one day” (2 Peter 3:8) — and that His delay is mercy: “The Lord is not slow concerning his promise, as some count slowness; but is patient with us, not wishing that any should perish, but that all should come to repentance” (2 Peter 3:9). The day will come, and so believers ask, ‘What kind of people ought we to be?’","This theme runs through the New Testament. Paul prays “that your love may abound yet more and more in knowledge and all discernment” (Philippians 1:9), and looks forward “until we all attain to the unity of the faith, and of the knowledge of the Son of God, to a full grown man” (Ephesians 4:13). Jesus warned that false prophets would come, and that the day would come like a thief. Peter, facing death, makes both truths his last word."]},"truths":{"intro":"As we journey through these 3 chapters, the Holy Spirit wants to anchor these truths in your spirit:","items":[{"title":"You Have Everything You Need to Grow","body":"God's divine power “has granted to us all things that pertain to life and godliness” (2 Peter 1:3). You do not lack resources; you need diligence. Peter calls believers to add virtue to virtue with “all diligence” (1:5), not to earn salvation, but to make their calling and election sure in experience. A fruitful life is the proof of a real calling."},{"title":"Truth Rests on Eyewitnesses and Scripture","body":"The gospel is not a clever myth. Peter saw Christ's majesty and heard the Father's voice (1:16–18). And beyond his own testimony stands Scripture: “For no prophecy ever came by the will of man: but holy men of God spoke, being moved by the Holy Spirit” (2 Peter 1:21). In a world of competing voices, believers anchor themselves in the apostolic witness and the inspired word."},{"title":"God's Patience Is Not God's Absence","body":"False teachers will come, scoffers will mock, and it may seem that God is slow. But God “knows how to deliver the godly out of temptation and to keep the unrighteous under punishment for the day of judgment” (2 Peter 2:9). His delay is patience, giving time for repentance. The day of the Lord will come, and with it “new heavens and a new earth, in which righteousness dwells” (2 Peter 3:13)."}]},"preparation":{"intro":"Before we step into Chapter 1, prepare your spirit with these reflections:","items":[{"title":"Are You Still Growing?","body":"When did you last grow noticeably in your knowledge of Christ, in self-control, in perseverance, in love? Peter warns that standing still is dangerous. Ask the Holy Spirit to show you which virtue He wants to add in this season, and read this letter with that in mind."},{"title":"Whose Voice Are You Listening To?","body":"Teachers, preachers, books, podcasts and social media fill your mind every day. Some build your faith; some subtly twist it. As you read 2 Peter, ask God to give you discernment: which voices are leading you deeper into Christ, and which are pulling you away from the truth or from holiness?"}]},"prayerOfActivation":"“Lord Jesus, as I open the second letter of Peter, I thank You that Your divine power has given me everything I need for life and godliness. Thank You for Your precious and very great promises, through which I share in Your nature and escape the corruption of the world. Make me diligent. Add to my faith virtue, knowledge, self-control, perseverance, godliness, brotherly affection and love, so that I am not idle or unfruitful in knowing You. Thank You for the eyewitnesses who saw Your majesty and for the Scriptures, spoken by holy men carried along by the Holy Spirit. Let Your word be a lamp in the dark places of my life until the day dawns and the morning star rises in my heart. Give me discernment to recognise false teaching, and keep me from being carried away by error. When it seems that You are slow, remind me that You are patient, not wanting anyone to perish. Make me holy and peaceful as I wait for new heavens and a new earth. Help me grow in Your grace and knowledge until that day. In Jesus' name, Amen.”"},"chapters":[{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"2 Peter","chapter":1,"chapterTitle":"Everything for Life and Godliness, and the Sure Word","themeTitle":"“His Divine Power Has Granted to Us All Things That Pertain to Life and Godliness”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"Simon Peter, a servant and apostle of Jesus Christ, to those who have obtained a like precious faith with us in the righteousness of our God and Savior, Jesus Christ:","understanding":"Συμεὼν Πέτρος, “Simon Peter” — many manuscripts spell it Συμεών, the old Hebrew form, Simeon, the name his family used. δοῦλος καὶ ἀπόστολος, “a servant and apostle of Jesus Christ” — δοῦλος, from the servant family: wholly owned, like James 1:1; ἀπόστολος, one sent with authority. τοῖς ἰσότιμον ἡμῖν λαχοῦσιν πίστιν, “to those who have obtained a like precious faith with us” — ἰσότιμος, of equal value: the faith of believers who never saw Jesus is as precious as the apostles' own; λαγχάνω, to receive by lot, as a gift, not earned. ἐν δικαιοσύνῃ τοῦ θεοῦ ἡμῶν καὶ σωτῆρος Ἰησοῦ Χριστοῦ, “in the righteousness of our God and Savior, Jesus Christ” — one article governs both nouns, as in Titus 2:13: Jesus is both God and Saviour.","words":[{"strongs":"G4826","hebrew":"Συμεών","translit":"Symeṓn","english":"Simon"},{"strongs":"G4074","hebrew":"Πέτρος","translit":"Pétros","english":"Peter"},{"strongs":"G1401","hebrew":"δοῦλος","translit":"doûlos","english":"servant"},{"strongs":"G652","hebrew":"ἀπόστολος","translit":"apóstolos","english":"apostle"},{"strongs":"G2975","hebrew":"λαγχάνω","translit":"lanchánō","english":"obtained"},{"strongs":"G2472","hebrew":"ἰσότιμος","translit":"isótimos","english":"like precious"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G1343","hebrew":"δικαιοσύνη","translit":"dikaiosýnē","english":"righteousness"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G4990","hebrew":"σωτήρ","translit":"sōtḗr","english":"Savior"}]},{"v":2,"text":"Grace to you and peace be multiplied in the knowledge of God and of Jesus our Lord,","understanding":"χάρις ὑμῖν καὶ εἰρήνη πληθυνθείη, “Grace to you and peace be multiplied” — the same greeting as 1 Peter 1:2. ἐν ἐπιγνώσει τοῦ θεοῦ καὶ Ἰησοῦ τοῦ κυρίου ἡμῶν, “in the knowledge of God and of Jesus our Lord” — ἐπίγνωσις, from the know family: full, personal, experiential knowledge, the relationship that saves, as distinct from γνῶσις (v. 5), knowledge as understanding. Grace and peace grow as knowledge of Christ grows.","words":[{"strongs":"G5485","hebrew":"χάρις","translit":"cháris","english":"Grace"},{"strongs":"G1515","hebrew":"εἰρήνη","translit":"eirḗnē","english":"peace"},{"strongs":"G4129","hebrew":"πληθύνω","translit":"plēthýnō","english":"multiplied"},{"strongs":"G1922","hebrew":"ἐπίγνωσις","translit":"epígnōsis","english":"knowledge"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"}]},{"v":3,"text":"seeing that his divine power has granted to us all things that pertain to life and godliness, through the knowledge of him who called us by his own glory and virtue;","understanding":"ὡς πάντα ἡμῖν τῆς θείας δυνάμεως αὐτοῦ τὰ πρὸς ζωὴν καὶ εὐσέβειαν δεδωρημένης, “seeing that his divine power has granted to us all things that pertain to life and godliness” — θεῖος, divine; δύναμις, from the power family: God's ability in action; δωρέομαι, from the gift family: to give freely as a gift; ζωή, from the life family; εὐσέβεια, from the godliness family: reverent devotion that shows in life. Nothing needed for spiritual life is missing. διὰ τῆς ἐπιγνώσεως τοῦ καλέσαντος ἡμᾶς, “through the knowledge of him who called us” — ἐπίγνωσις again, from the know family. ἰδίᾳ δόξῃ καὶ ἀρετῇ, “by his own glory and virtue” — ἀρετή, excellence, the quality Peter calls believers to add in verse 5.","words":[{"strongs":"G2304","hebrew":"θεῖος","translit":"theîos","english":"divine"},{"strongs":"G1411","hebrew":"δύναμις","translit":"dýnamis","english":"power"},{"strongs":"G1433","hebrew":"δωρέομαι","translit":"dōréomai","english":"granted"},{"strongs":"G2222","hebrew":"ζωή","translit":"zōḗ","english":"life"},{"strongs":"G2150","hebrew":"εὐσέβεια","translit":"eusébeia","english":"godliness"},{"strongs":"G1922","hebrew":"ἐπίγνωσις","translit":"epígnōsis","english":"knowledge"},{"strongs":"G2564","hebrew":"καλέω","translit":"kaléō","english":"called"},{"strongs":"G1391","hebrew":"δόξα","translit":"dóxa","english":"glory"},{"strongs":"G703","hebrew":"ἀρέτη","translit":"arétē","english":"virtue"}]},{"v":4,"text":"by which he has granted to us his precious and exceedingly great promises; that through these you may become partakers of the divine nature, having escaped from the corruption that is in the world by lust.","understanding":"δι᾽ ὧν τὰ τίμια καὶ μέγιστα ἡμῖν ἐπαγγέλματα δεδώρηται, “by which he has granted to us his precious and exceedingly great promises” — δωρέομαι again, from the gift family; τίμιος, the ‘precious’ of 1 Peter 1:19; ἐπάγγελμα, a promise. ἵνα διὰ τούτων γένησθε θείας κοινωνοὶ φύσεως, “that through these you may become partakers of the divine nature” — κοινωνός, a sharer; φύσις, nature. Not becoming God, but sharing His moral character and life by the Spirit. ἀποφυγόντες τῆς ἐν τῷ κόσμῳ ἐν ἐπιθυμίᾳ φθορᾶς, “having escaped from the corruption that is in the world by lust” — κόσμος, from the world family; φθορά, decay, rot, which desire brings.","words":[{"strongs":"G5093","hebrew":"τίμιος","translit":"tímios","english":"precious"},{"strongs":"G3176","hebrew":"μέγιστος","translit":"mégistos","english":"exceedingly great"},{"strongs":"G1862","hebrew":"ἐπάγγελμα","translit":"epángelma","english":"promises"},{"strongs":"G1433","hebrew":"δωρέομαι","translit":"dōréomai","english":"granted"},{"strongs":"G2844","hebrew":"κοινωνός","translit":"koinōnós","english":"partakers"},{"strongs":"G2304","hebrew":"θεῖος","translit":"theîos","english":"divine"},{"strongs":"G5449","hebrew":"φύσις","translit":"phýsis","english":"nature"},{"strongs":"G668","hebrew":"ἀποφεύγω","translit":"apopheúgō","english":"escaped"},{"strongs":"G5356","hebrew":"φθορά","translit":"phthorá","english":"corruption"},{"strongs":"G2889","hebrew":"κόσμος","translit":"kósmos","english":"world"},{"strongs":"G1939","hebrew":"ἐπιθυμία","translit":"epithymía","english":"lust"}]},{"v":5,"text":"Yes, and for this very cause adding on your part all diligence, in your faith supply moral excellence; and in moral excellence, knowledge;","understanding":"καὶ αὐτὸ τοῦτο δὲ σπουδὴν πᾶσαν παρεισενέγκαντες, “Yes, and for this very cause adding on your part all diligence” — σπουδή, earnest effort. God gave everything (v. 3); now you bring everything. ἐπιχορηγήσατε ἐν τῇ πίστει ὑμῶν τὴν ἀρετήν, “in your faith supply moral excellence” — ἐπιχορηγέω, to supply lavishly, from the χορηγός who paid for a chorus; the same verb returns in verse 11. ἀρετή, the ‘virtue’ of verse 3: the WEB uses different English for the same Greek. ἐν δὲ τῇ ἀρετῇ τὴν γνῶσιν, “and in moral excellence, knowledge” — γνῶσις, from the know family: practical understanding, the knowledge that grows, as distinct from ἐπίγνωσις (vv. 2–3), the knowledge that saves.","words":[{"strongs":"G4710","hebrew":"σπουδή","translit":"spoudḗ","english":"diligence"},{"strongs":"G3923","hebrew":"παρεισφέρω","translit":"pareisphérō","english":"adding"},{"strongs":"G2023","hebrew":"ἐπιχορηγέω","translit":"epichorēgéō","english":"supply"},{"strongs":"G4102","hebrew":"πίστις","translit":"pístis","english":"faith"},{"strongs":"G703","hebrew":"ἀρέτη","translit":"arétē","english":"moral excellence"},{"strongs":"G1108","hebrew":"γνῶσις","translit":"gnōsis","english":"knowledge"}]},{"v":6,"text":"and in knowledge, self-control; and in self-control perseverance; and in perseverance godliness;","understanding":"ἐν δὲ τῇ γνώσει τὴν ἐγκράτειαν, “and in knowledge, self-control” — γνῶσις, from the know family, then ἐγκράτεια, from the self-control family: mastery of oneself, holding desires in check, as distinct from σωφροσύνη, sound-mindedness. ἐν δὲ τῇ ἐγκρατείᾳ τὴν ὑπομονήν, “and in self-control perseverance” — ὑπομονή, from the endurance family: remaining under a load, the word of James 1:3. ἐν δὲ τῇ ὑπομονῇ τὴν εὐσέβειαν, “and in perseverance godliness” — εὐσέβεια, from the godliness family, as in verse 3. Knowledge without self-control produces the false teachers of chapter 2.","words":[{"strongs":"G1108","hebrew":"γνῶσις","translit":"gnōsis","english":"knowledge"},{"strongs":"G1466","hebrew":"ἐγκράτεια","translit":"enkráteia","english":"self-control"},{"strongs":"G5281","hebrew":"ὑπομονή","translit":"hypomonḗ","english":"perseverance"},{"strongs":"G2150","hebrew":"εὐσέβεια","translit":"eusébeia","english":"godliness"}]},{"v":7,"text":"and in godliness brotherly affection; and in brotherly affection, love.","understanding":"ἐν δὲ τῇ εὐσεβείᾳ τὴν φιλαδελφίαν, “and in godliness brotherly affection” — εὐσέβεια, from the godliness family, then φιλαδελφία, from the love family: the warm affection of brothers and sisters. ἐν δὲ τῇ φιλαδελφίᾳ τὴν ἀγάπην, “and in brotherly affection, love” — ἀγάπη, also from the love family: self-giving love that reaches beyond the family to all. The ladder ends where Peter's first letter put both side by side (1 Peter 1:22): φιλαδελφία must grow into ἀγάπη. Love is the crown of all the virtues (Colossians 3:14).","words":[{"strongs":"G2150","hebrew":"εὐσέβεια","translit":"eusébeia","english":"godliness"},{"strongs":"G5360","hebrew":"φιλαδελφία","translit":"philadelphía","english":"brotherly affection"},{"strongs":"G26","hebrew":"ἀγάπη","translit":"agápē","english":"love"}]},{"v":8,"text":"For if these things are yours and abound, they make you to be not idle or unfruitful in the knowledge of our Lord Jesus Christ.","understanding":"ταῦτα γὰρ ὑμῖν ὑπάρχοντα καὶ πλεονάζοντα, “For if these things are yours and abound” — πλεονάζω, to increase, to be more and more. οὐκ ἀργοὺς οὐδὲ ἀκάρπους καθίστησιν, “they make you to be not idle or unfruitful” — ἀργός, idle, ‘no-work’, the very word many manuscripts read in James 2:20 for faith without works; ἄκαρπος, fruitless. εἰς τὴν τοῦ κυρίου ἡμῶν Ἰησοῦ Χριστοῦ ἐπίγνωσιν, “in the knowledge of our Lord Jesus Christ” — ἐπίγνωσις, from the know family: growth in virtue leads into deeper personal knowledge of Christ.","words":[{"strongs":"G5225","hebrew":"ὑπάρχω","translit":"hypárchō","english":"are yours"},{"strongs":"G4121","hebrew":"πλεονάζω","translit":"pleonázō","english":"abound"},{"strongs":"G692","hebrew":"ἀργός","translit":"argós","english":"idle"},{"strongs":"G175","hebrew":"ἄκαρπος","translit":"ákarpos","english":"unfruitful"},{"strongs":"G1922","hebrew":"ἐπίγνωσις","translit":"epígnōsis","english":"knowledge"}]},{"v":9,"text":"For he who lacks these things is blind, seeing only what is near, having forgotten the cleansing from his old sins.","understanding":"ᾧ γὰρ μὴ πάρεστιν ταῦτα, τυφλός ἐστιν μυωπάζων, “For he who lacks these things is blind, seeing only what is near” — τυφλός, blind; μυωπάζω, to be short-sighted, to squint: he sees only the present and cannot see eternity. λήθην λαβὼν τοῦ καθαρισμοῦ τῶν πάλαι αὐτοῦ ἁμαρτιῶν, “having forgotten the cleansing from his old sins” — λήθη, forgetfulness; καθαρισμός, cleansing, the word Hebrews uses of Christ's work (Hebrews 1:3); ἁμαρτία, from the sin family. Spiritual stagnation begins with forgetting what Christ has done.","words":[{"strongs":"G3918","hebrew":"πάρειμι","translit":"páreimi","english":"lacks"},{"strongs":"G5185","hebrew":"τυφλός","translit":"typhlós","english":"blind"},{"strongs":"G3467","hebrew":"μυωπάζω","translit":"myōpázō","english":"seeing only what is near"},{"strongs":"G3024","hebrew":"λήθη","translit":"lḗthē","english":"forgotten"},{"strongs":"G2512","hebrew":"καθαρισμός","translit":"katharismós","english":"cleansing"},{"strongs":"G3819","hebrew":"πάλαι","translit":"pálai","english":"old"},{"strongs":"G266","hebrew":"ἁμαρτία","translit":"hamartía","english":"sins"}]},{"v":10,"text":"Therefore, brothers, be more diligent to make your calling and election sure. For if you do these things, you will never stumble.","understanding":"διὸ μᾶλλον, ἀδελφοί, σπουδάσατε, “Therefore, brothers, be more diligent” — σπουδάζω, the verb of σπουδή (v. 5). βεβαίαν ὑμῶν τὴν κλῆσιν καὶ ἐκλογὴν ποιεῖσθαι, “to make your calling and election sure” — βέβαιος, firm, guaranteed, a legal word for a confirmed title; κλῆσις, God's call; ἐκλογή, His choice. A growing life does not create God's choice; it confirms it in experience. ταῦτα γὰρ ποιοῦντες οὐ μὴ πταίσητέ ποτε, “For if you do these things, you will never stumble” — πταίω, to trip, the word James uses for stumbling in one point of the law and in speech.","words":[{"strongs":"G80","hebrew":"ἀδελφός","translit":"adelphós","english":"brothers"},{"strongs":"G4704","hebrew":"σπουδάζω","translit":"spoudázō","english":"be more diligent"},{"strongs":"G949","hebrew":"βέβαιος","translit":"bébaios","english":"sure"},{"strongs":"G2821","hebrew":"κλῆσις","translit":"klēsis","english":"calling"},{"strongs":"G1589","hebrew":"ἐκλογή","translit":"eklogḗ","english":"election"},{"strongs":"G4160","hebrew":"ποιέω","translit":"poiéō","english":"do"},{"strongs":"G4417","hebrew":"πταίω","translit":"ptaíō","english":"stumble"}]},{"v":11,"text":"For thus you will be richly supplied with the entrance into the eternal Kingdom of our Lord and Savior, Jesus Christ.","understanding":"οὕτως γὰρ πλουσίως ἐπιχορηγηθήσεται ὑμῖν ἡ εἴσοδος, “For thus you will be richly supplied with the entrance” — ἐπιχορηγέω again, as in verse 5: supply virtue generously, and God will generously supply your welcome. πλουσίως, richly; εἴσοδος, the way in. εἰς τὴν αἰώνιον βασιλείαν τοῦ κυρίου ἡμῶν καὶ σωτῆρος Ἰησοῦ Χριστοῦ, “into the eternal Kingdom of our Lord and Savior, Jesus Christ” — αἰώνιος, from the time family; σωτήρ, as in verse 1.","words":[{"strongs":"G4146","hebrew":"πλουσίως","translit":"plousíōs","english":"richly"},{"strongs":"G2023","hebrew":"ἐπιχορηγέω","translit":"epichorēgéō","english":"supplied"},{"strongs":"G1529","hebrew":"εἴσοδος","translit":"eísodos","english":"entrance"},{"strongs":"G166","hebrew":"αἰώνιος","translit":"aiṓnios","english":"eternal"},{"strongs":"G932","hebrew":"βασιλεία","translit":"basileía","english":"Kingdom"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G4990","hebrew":"σωτήρ","translit":"sōtḗr","english":"Savior"}]},{"v":12,"text":"Therefore I will not be negligent to remind you of these things, though you know them, and are established in the present truth.","understanding":"διὸ μελλήσω ἀεὶ ὑμᾶς ὑπομιμνῄσκειν περὶ τούτων, “Therefore I will not be negligent to remind you of these things” — ὑπομιμνῄσκω, to remind, the theme of verses 12–15. Believers need reminding more than new information. καίπερ εἰδότας καὶ ἐστηριγμένους ἐν τῇ παρούσῃ ἀληθείᾳ, “though you know them, and are established in the present truth” — εἴδω, from the know family (and the see family); στηρίζω, to make firm, the very word Jesus used: ‘establish your brothers’ (Luke 22:32). Peter is still obeying.","words":[{"strongs":"G272","hebrew":"ἀμελέω","translit":"ameléō","english":"negligent"},{"strongs":"G5279","hebrew":"ὑπομιμνήσκω","translit":"hypomimnḗskō","english":"remind"},{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"know"},{"strongs":"G4741","hebrew":"στηρίζω","translit":"stērízō","english":"established"},{"strongs":"G3918","hebrew":"πάρειμι","translit":"páreimi","english":"present"},{"strongs":"G225","hebrew":"ἀλήθεια","translit":"alḗtheia","english":"truth"}]},{"v":13,"text":"I think it right, as long as I am in this tent, to stir you up by reminding you;","understanding":"δίκαιον δὲ ἡγοῦμαι, “I think it right” — ἡγέομαι, the considered judgement of James 1:2. ἐφ᾽ ὅσον εἰμὶ ἐν τούτῳ τῷ σκηνώματι, “as long as I am in this tent” — σκήνωμα, a tent: the body as a temporary dwelling, the language Paul uses (2 Corinthians 5:1). διεγείρειν ὑμᾶς ἐν ὑπομνήσει, “to stir you up by reminding you” — διεγείρω, to wake fully, the word used of the disciples waking Jesus in the storm.","words":[{"strongs":"G2233","hebrew":"ἡγέομαι","translit":"hēgéomai","english":"think"},{"strongs":"G1342","hebrew":"δίκαιος","translit":"díkaios","english":"right"},{"strongs":"G4638","hebrew":"σκήνωμα","translit":"skḗnōma","english":"tent"},{"strongs":"G1326","hebrew":"διεγείρω","translit":"diegeírō","english":"stir you up"},{"strongs":"G5280","hebrew":"ὑπόμνησις","translit":"hypómnēsis","english":"reminding"}]},{"v":14,"text":"knowing that the putting off of my tent comes swiftly, even as our Lord Jesus Christ made clear to me.","understanding":"εἰδὼς ὅτι ταχινή ἐστιν ἡ ἀπόθεσις τοῦ σκηνώματός μου, “knowing that the putting off of my tent comes swiftly” — εἴδω, from the know family (and the see family): knowing as settled fact; ἀπόθεσις, the ‘putting away’ of 1 Peter 3:21, here taking off the body like a garment or folding a tent; ταχινός, soon, or suddenly. καθὼς καὶ ὁ κύριος ἡμῶν Ἰησοῦς Χριστὸς ἐδήλωσέν μοι, “even as our Lord Jesus Christ made clear to me” — δηλόω, to make plain. Jesus had told him by the lake how he would die (John 21:18–19).","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"knowing"},{"strongs":"G595","hebrew":"ἀπόθεσις","translit":"apóthesis","english":"putting off"},{"strongs":"G4638","hebrew":"σκήνωμα","translit":"skḗnōma","english":"tent"},{"strongs":"G5031","hebrew":"ταχινός","translit":"tachinós","english":"swiftly"},{"strongs":"G1213","hebrew":"δηλόω","translit":"dēlóō","english":"made clear"}]},{"v":15,"text":"Yes, I will make every effort that you may always be able to remember these things even after my departure.","understanding":"σπουδάσω δὲ καὶ ἑκάστοτε ἔχειν ὑμᾶς μετὰ τὴν ἐμὴν ἔξοδον, “Yes, I will make every effort that you may always be able to remember these things even after my departure” — σπουδάζω, as in verse 10; ἔξοδος, ‘exodus’, departure — the very word Moses and Elijah used on the mountain of transfiguration for Jesus' death (Luke 9:31). τὴν τούτων μνήμην ποιεῖσθαι, to keep these things in memory. Early tradition says Mark's Gospel preserved Peter's preaching; this letter is part of his effort.","words":[{"strongs":"G4704","hebrew":"σπουδάζω","translit":"spoudázō","english":"make every effort"},{"strongs":"G1841","hebrew":"ἔξοδος","translit":"éxodos","english":"departure"},{"strongs":"G3420","hebrew":"μνήμη","translit":"mnḗmē","english":"remember"}]},{"v":16,"text":"For we did not follow cunningly devised fables, when we made known to you the power and coming of our Lord Jesus Christ, but we were eyewitnesses of his majesty.","understanding":"οὐ γὰρ σεσοφισμένοις μύθοις ἐξακολουθήσαντες, “For we did not follow cunningly devised fables” — σοφίζω, to make clever, to contrive; μῦθος, a myth, a made-up story, like the tales of the Greek gods. ἐγνωρίσαμεν ὑμῖν τὴν τοῦ κυρίου ἡμῶν Ἰησοῦ Χριστοῦ δύναμιν καὶ παρουσίαν, “when we made known to you the power and coming of our Lord Jesus Christ” — δύναμις, from the power family; παρουσία, His royal coming, which the scoffers deny (3:4). ἀλλ᾽ ἐπόπται γενηθέντες τῆς ἐκείνου μεγαλειότητος, “but we were eyewitnesses of his majesty” — ἐπόπτης, from the see family: one who has seen for himself, the noun of ἐποπτεύω (1 Peter 2:12); μεγαλειότης, greatness, splendour.","words":[{"strongs":"G4679","hebrew":"σοφίζω","translit":"sophízō","english":"cunningly devised"},{"strongs":"G3454","hebrew":"μῦθος","translit":"mŷthos","english":"fables"},{"strongs":"G1811","hebrew":"ἐξακολουθέω","translit":"exakolouthéō","english":"follow"},{"strongs":"G1107","hebrew":"γνωρίζω","translit":"gnōrízō","english":"made known"},{"strongs":"G1411","hebrew":"δύναμις","translit":"dýnamis","english":"power"},{"strongs":"G3952","hebrew":"παρουσία","translit":"parousía","english":"coming"},{"strongs":"G2030","hebrew":"ἐπόπτης","translit":"epóptēs","english":"eyewitnesses"},{"strongs":"G3168","hebrew":"μεγαλειότης","translit":"megaleiótēs","english":"majesty"}]},{"v":17,"text":"For he received from God the Father honor and glory, when the voice came to him from the Majestic Glory, “This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased.”","understanding":"λαβὼν γὰρ παρὰ θεοῦ πατρὸς τιμὴν καὶ δόξαν, “For he received from God the Father honor and glory” — τιμή and δόξα. φωνῆς ἐνεχθείσης αὐτῷ τοιᾶσδε ὑπὸ τῆς μεγαλοπρεποῦς δόξης, “when the voice came to him from the Majestic Glory” — φέρω, ‘borne’, a word Peter will repeat in verse 21; μεγαλοπρεπής, magnificent, a reverent name for God. “This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased.” — ἀγαπητός, from the love family; εὐδοκέω, to take delight. The same words were heard at Jesus' baptism and on the mountain (Matthew 17:5).","words":[{"strongs":"G2983","hebrew":"λαμβάνω","translit":"lambánō","english":"received"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G3962","hebrew":"πατήρ","translit":"patḗr","english":"Father"},{"strongs":"G5092","hebrew":"τιμή","translit":"timḗ","english":"honor"},{"strongs":"G1391","hebrew":"δόξα","translit":"dóxa","english":"glory"},{"strongs":"G5456","hebrew":"φωνή","translit":"phōnḗ","english":"voice"},{"strongs":"G5342","hebrew":"φέρω","translit":"phérō","english":"came"},{"strongs":"G3169","hebrew":"μεγαλοπρεπής","translit":"megaloprepḗs","english":"Majestic"},{"strongs":"G5207","hebrew":"υἱός","translit":"huiós","english":"Son"},{"strongs":"G27","hebrew":"ἀγαπητός","translit":"agapētós","english":"beloved"},{"strongs":"G2106","hebrew":"εὐδοκέω","translit":"eudokéō","english":"well pleased"}]},{"v":18,"text":"We heard this voice come out of heaven when we were with him on the holy mountain.","understanding":"καὶ ταύτην τὴν φωνὴν ἡμεῖς ἠκούσαμεν, “We heard this voice” — ἀκούω: seeing (v. 16) and hearing. ἐξ οὐρανοῦ ἐνεχθεῖσαν, “come out of heaven” — φέρω again. σὺν αὐτῷ ὄντες ἐν τῷ ἁγίῳ ὄρει, “when we were with him on the holy mountain” — ὄρος, the mountain, holy because God's presence came there, like Sinai. Peter, James and John were there (Luke 9:28).","words":[{"strongs":"G191","hebrew":"ἀκούω","translit":"akoúō","english":"heard"},{"strongs":"G5456","hebrew":"φωνή","translit":"phōnḗ","english":"voice"},{"strongs":"G5342","hebrew":"φέρω","translit":"phérō","english":"come"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heaven"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"holy"},{"strongs":"G3735","hebrew":"ὄρος","translit":"óros","english":"mountain"}]},{"v":19,"text":"We have the more sure word of prophecy; and you do well that you heed it, as to a lamp shining in a dark place, until the day dawns, and the morning star arises in your hearts:","understanding":"καὶ ἔχομεν βεβαιότερον τὸν προφητικὸν λόγον, “We have the more sure word of prophecy” — βέβαιος, the ‘sure’ of verse 10; προφητικός, prophetic; λόγος, from the word family. The transfiguration confirmed the prophets; now their word is even more certain. ᾧ καλῶς ποιεῖτε προσέχοντες, “and you do well that you heed it” — καλῶς, from the good family. ὡς λύχνῳ φαίνοντι ἐν αὐχμηρῷ τόπῳ, “as to a lamp shining in a dark place” — φαίνω, from the appear family: to shine; αὐχμηρός, dry, dirty, murky. ἕως οὗ ἡμέρα διαυγάσῃ, “until the day dawns” — ἡμέρα, from the time family. καὶ φωσφόρος ἀνατείλῃ ἐν ταῖς καρδίαις ὑμῶν, “and the morning star arises in your hearts” — φωσφόρος, ‘light-bearer’, the morning star; Christ calls Himself ‘the Bright and Morning Star’ (Revelation 22:16).","words":[{"strongs":"G949","hebrew":"βέβαιος","translit":"bébaios","english":"more sure"},{"strongs":"G4397","hebrew":"προφητικός","translit":"prophētikós","english":"of prophecy"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"word"},{"strongs":"G2573","hebrew":"καλῶς","translit":"kalōs","english":"well"},{"strongs":"G4337","hebrew":"προσέχω","translit":"proséchō","english":"heed"},{"strongs":"G3088","hebrew":"λύχνος","translit":"lýchnos","english":"lamp"},{"strongs":"G5316","hebrew":"φαίνω","translit":"phaínō","english":"shining"},{"strongs":"G850","hebrew":"αὐχμηρός","translit":"auchmērós","english":"dark"},{"strongs":"G2250","hebrew":"ἡμέρα","translit":"hēméra","english":"day"},{"strongs":"G1306","hebrew":"διαυγάζω","translit":"diaugázō","english":"dawns"},{"strongs":"G5459","hebrew":"φωσφόρος","translit":"phōsphóros","english":"morning star"},{"strongs":"G393","hebrew":"ἀνατέλλω","translit":"anatéllō","english":"arises"},{"strongs":"G2588","hebrew":"καρδία","translit":"kardía","english":"hearts"}]},{"v":20,"text":"knowing this first, that no prophecy of Scripture is of private interpretation.","understanding":"τοῦτο πρῶτον γινώσκοντες, “knowing this first” — γινώσκω, from the know family: to know by experience and understanding. ὅτι πᾶσα προφητεία γραφῆς ἰδίας ἐπιλύσεως οὐ γίνεται, “that no prophecy of Scripture is of private interpretation” — ἐπίλυσις, an untying, an explanation; ἴδιος, one's own. In context (v. 21) the strongest reading is that no prophecy came from the prophet's own interpretation of things; its origin was God. It also implies that Scripture must not be twisted to private meanings (3:16).","words":[{"strongs":"G1097","hebrew":"γινώσκω","translit":"ginṓskō","english":"knowing"},{"strongs":"G4394","hebrew":"προφητεία","translit":"prophēteía","english":"prophecy"},{"strongs":"G1124","hebrew":"γραφή","translit":"graphḗ","english":"Scripture"},{"strongs":"G2398","hebrew":"ἴδιος","translit":"ídios","english":"private"},{"strongs":"G1955","hebrew":"ἐπίλυσις","translit":"epílysis","english":"interpretation"}]},{"v":21,"text":"For no prophecy ever came by the will of man: but holy men of God spoke, being moved by the Holy Spirit.","understanding":"οὐ γὰρ θελήματι ἀνθρώπου ἠνέχθη προφητεία ποτέ, “For no prophecy ever came by the will of man” — φέρω, ‘was borne’, the verb of verses 17–18. ἀλλὰ ὑπὸ πνεύματος ἁγίου φερόμενοι ἐλάλησαν ἀπὸ θεοῦ ἄνθρωποι, “but holy men of God spoke, being moved by the Holy Spirit” — φέρω once more: ‘carried along’, as a ship is carried by the wind (Acts 27:15, 17). The voice was borne from heaven; prophecy was not borne by human will; the prophets were borne along by the Spirit. Paul says the same: “Every Scripture is God-breathed” (2 Timothy 3:16).","words":[{"strongs":"G2307","hebrew":"θέλημα","translit":"thélēma","english":"will"},{"strongs":"G444","hebrew":"ἄνθρωπος","translit":"ánthrōpos","english":"man"},{"strongs":"G5342","hebrew":"φέρω","translit":"phérō","english":"moved"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"Holy"},{"strongs":"G4151","hebrew":"πνεῦμα","translit":"pneûma","english":"Spirit"},{"strongs":"G2980","hebrew":"λαλέω","translit":"laléō","english":"spoke"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G4394","hebrew":"προφητεία","translit":"prophēteía","english":"prophecy"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"The Ladder of Growth","verseRef":"v. 5-8","image":"Picture a young vine planted against a wall. The gardener has given it rich soil, sun and water — everything it needs. Year by year it climbs, adding branch upon branch: first a sturdy stem, then side shoots, then leaves, then blossoms, then clusters of grapes. A vine that never climbs, never branches and never fruits is only a stick in the ground.","meaning":"God's power has given us everything for life and godliness (v. 3). Now Peter says, “in your faith supply moral excellence; and in moral excellence, knowledge” (v. 5), “and in knowledge, self-control; and in self-control perseverance; and in perseverance godliness” (v. 6), “and in godliness brotherly affection; and in brotherly affection, love” (v. 7). “For if these things are yours and abound, they make you to be not idle or unfruitful in the knowledge of our Lord Jesus Christ” (v. 8). Growth is not optional for a living vine."},{"title":"The Folded Tent","verseRef":"v. 13-15","image":"Picture an old shepherd at dawn, folding his tent for the last time. He has lived in it through many seasons, but now he is moving on to a permanent house. Before he goes, he gathers the younger shepherds around him and tells them again everything he has taught them, so that they will remember after he is gone.","meaning":"“I think it right, as long as I am in this tent, to stir you up by reminding you” (v. 13), “knowing that the putting off of my tent comes swiftly, even as our Lord Jesus Christ made clear to me” (v. 14). Peter writes as a man about to die, and he spends his last effort reminding believers of the truths they already know. What you most need is often not something new, but a fresh grip on what you have already been taught."},{"title":"Light on the Mountain","verseRef":"v. 16-18","image":"Picture three fishermen on a mountain at night, heavy with sleep. Suddenly they wake to see their teacher shining with light brighter than the sun, His clothes dazzling white, and two figures from long ago talking with Him. A bright cloud covers them, and a voice from the cloud says, ‘This is my beloved Son.’ They fall on their faces in terror.","meaning":"“For we did not follow cunningly devised fables, when we made known to you the power and coming of our Lord Jesus Christ, but we were eyewitnesses of his majesty” (v. 16). “We heard this voice come out of heaven when we were with him on the holy mountain” (v. 18). The Christian faith is not a clever story invented by people. It rests on what real people saw and heard. Peter staked his life — and his death — on it."},{"title":"The Lamp in the Dark","verseRef":"v. 19-21","image":"Picture a traveller in a gloomy, dirty alley at night, holding a small oil lamp. Its circle of light is not large, but it shows him where to step. He keeps walking, guided by the lamp, until the sky behind the rooftops begins to grey, and then the morning star appears, and then the sun rises and floods the whole city with light.","meaning":"“We have the more sure word of prophecy; and you do well that you heed it, as to a lamp shining in a dark place, until the day dawns, and the morning star arises in your hearts” (v. 19). Scripture is your lamp in a dark world. It came not from human will, “but holy men of God spoke, being moved by the Holy Spirit” (v. 21). Keep walking by its light until the Day dawns and Christ Himself appears."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"Greeting (v. 1-2)","body":"Simon Peter, servant and apostle, writes to those who share an equally precious faith through the righteousness of our God and Saviour Jesus Christ; may grace and peace be multiplied through the knowledge of God and Jesus."},{"label":"God's Provision and Our Diligence (v. 3-11)","body":"God's divine power has given everything needed for life and godliness through knowing Him, and through His promises we share the divine nature, having escaped the world's corruption; so make every effort to add to faith virtue, knowledge, self-control, perseverance, godliness, brotherly affection and love; these qualities keep you fruitful, while their absence makes you short-sighted and forgetful; be diligent to confirm your calling and election, and you will receive a rich welcome into Christ's eternal kingdom."},{"label":"Peter's Reminder Before His Death (v. 12-15)","body":"Peter will keep reminding them, though they know these truths, because he knows his death is near, as the Lord showed him, and he wants them to remember after his departure."},{"label":"Eyewitnesses and Prophets (v. 16-21)","body":"The apostles did not follow clever myths but were eyewitnesses of Christ's majesty on the holy mountain, where they heard the Father's voice; the prophetic word is made more certain, a lamp in a dark place until the day dawns; no prophecy came from human will, but people spoke from God as they were carried along by the Holy Spirit."}],"sections":[{"heading":"Everything You Need, and Everything You Must Add","paragraphs":["Peter opens with a staggering claim: “seeing that his divine power has granted to us all things that pertain to life and godliness, through the knowledge of him who called us by his own glory and virtue” (v. 3). Believers are not spiritual beggars waiting for some missing ingredient. In knowing Christ they already have every resource for spiritual life and godly living. And through God's “precious and exceedingly great promises” (v. 4) they share the divine nature — not becoming God, but receiving His life and reflecting His character by the Spirit, having escaped the corruption that desire produces.","But God's provision does not make us passive. “Yes, and for this very cause adding on your part all diligence” (v. 5). Because God has given everything, we must give everything. Peter then lists eight qualities in a chain, each growing out of the one before: faith, moral excellence, knowledge, self-control, perseverance, godliness, brotherly affection and love. Faith is the root; love is the crown. Paul's fruit of the Spirit makes the same point: “But the fruit of the Spirit is love, joy, peace, patience, kindness, goodness, faith” (Galatians 5:22).","Two warnings follow. Growth produces fruit: “For if these things are yours and abound, they make you to be not idle or unfruitful in the knowledge of our Lord Jesus Christ” (v. 8). Its absence produces blindness: “For he who lacks these things is blind, seeing only what is near, having forgotten the cleansing from his old sins” (v. 9). A Christian who stops growing becomes short-sighted — fixed on the present, unable to see eternity — and forgetful of the grace that saved him. That is exactly the kind of person false teachers find easy to deceive."]},{"heading":"Making Your Calling Sure","paragraphs":["“Therefore, brothers, be more diligent to make your calling and election sure. For if you do these things, you will never stumble” (v. 10). Peter does not mean that our efforts create God's choice of us — He called us “by his own glory and virtue” (v. 3). He means that a growing, fruitful life confirms that the call was real, both to ourselves and to others. James said the same about faith and works. Assurance grows as godliness grows.","The reward is a generous welcome: “For thus you will be richly supplied with the entrance into the eternal Kingdom of our Lord and Savior, Jesus Christ” (v. 11). The same verb — ἐπιχορηγέω, to supply lavishly — is used in verse 5 and verse 11. Supply virtue generously, and God will supply your entrance generously. Some will be saved ‘as through fire’; Peter wants his readers to enter the kingdom with a rich welcome.","Then Peter explains why he keeps repeating himself: he is about to die. “knowing that the putting off of my tent comes swiftly, even as our Lord Jesus Christ made clear to me” (v. 14). Jesus had told him: “But when you are old, you will stretch out your hands, and another will dress you, and carry you where you don’t want to go” (John 21:18). Peter calls his death his ‘exodus’ (v. 15), the same word used of Jesus' death on the mountain of transfiguration: Moses and Elijah “spoke of his departure, which he was about to accomplish at Jerusalem” (Luke 9:31). Peter goes to his own departure following his Lord."]},{"heading":"Not Fables, but Eyewitnesses and Prophets","paragraphs":["False teachers apparently claimed that the apostles' message — especially about Christ's power and return — was a myth. Peter answers with testimony: “For we did not follow cunningly devised fables, when we made known to you the power and coming of our Lord Jesus Christ, but we were eyewitnesses of his majesty” (v. 16). On the mountain he saw Christ's glory and heard the Father's voice: “This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased” (v. 17). The transfiguration was a preview of the coming kingdom. John says the same: “We saw his glory, such glory as of the one and only Son of the Father, full of grace and truth” (John 1:14).","Then Peter points beyond his own experience to Scripture: “We have the more sure word of prophecy; and you do well that you heed it, as to a lamp shining in a dark place, until the day dawns, and the morning star arises in your hearts” (v. 19). The transfiguration confirmed what the prophets had said. Their word is a lamp in a murky world, enough to walk by until Christ returns and fills the heart with full light.","Finally, Peter explains why Scripture is trustworthy: “For no prophecy ever came by the will of man: but holy men of God spoke, being moved by the Holy Spirit” (v. 21). The prophets were real people who spoke real words, but they were ‘carried along’ by the Spirit like a ship by the wind. Paul says, “Every Scripture is God-breathed” (2 Timothy 3:16). That is why Scripture must not be bent to private interpretations (v. 20), and why it is the believer's final defence against false teaching (chapter 2)."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"The Transfiguration Remembered","paragraphs":["The Gospels record the event Peter remembers. Jesus “took with him Peter, John, and James, and went up onto the mountain to pray” (Luke 9:28). Moses and Elijah “appeared in glory, and spoke of his departure, which he was about to accomplish at Jerusalem” (Luke 9:31). “Now Peter and those who were with him were heavy with sleep, but when they were fully awake, they saw his glory” (Luke 9:32). And “A voice came out of the cloud, saying, ‘This is my beloved Son. Listen to him!’” (Luke 9:35). Matthew records the words Peter quotes: “This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased. Listen to him” (Matthew 17:5).","On the mountain Peter had wanted to build three shelters — tents — to keep the moment. Now, years later, he speaks of his own body as a tent about to be folded (vv. 13–14), and of his death as an exodus. Paul uses the same picture: “For we know that if the earthly house of our tent is dissolved, we have a building from God, a house not made with hands, eternal, in the heavens” (2 Corinthians 5:1).","The morning star of verse 19 shines at the end of the Bible: “I, Jesus, have sent my angel to testify these things to you for the assemblies. I am the root and the offspring of David; the Bright and Morning Star” (Revelation 22:16). And the ladder of virtues finds its summit where Paul places it too: “But now faith, hope, and love remain—these three. The greatest of these is love” (1 Corinthians 13:13)."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Knowledge","hebrew":"ἐπίγνωσις","translit":"epígnōsis","strongs":"G1922 (knowledge γνῶσις, gnōsis, G1108)","verses":"v. 2, 3, 8; 1:5-6 (γνῶσις); 2:20; 3:18","insight":"Strong's: “recognition, i.e. (by implication) full discernment, acknowledgement.” ἐπίγνωσις is the key word of 2 Peter. It is the personal, relational knowledge of God and Christ that saves and transforms (vv. 2, 3, 8). γνῶσις (vv. 5–6) is understanding that grows as part of the Christian life. Both are in the know family. The false teachers had a kind of knowledge, but not ἐπίγνωσις that produces godliness. Peter's last words are ‘grow in the grace and knowledge’ (3:18)."},{"english":"Supply","hebrew":"ἐπιχορηγέω","translit":"epichorēgéō","strongs":"G2023","verses":"v. 5, 11","insight":"Strong's: “to furnish besides, i.e. fully supply, (figuratively) aid or contribute.” The word comes from the χορηγός, the wealthy citizen who paid all the costs of a chorus at a Greek festival — and did it lavishly. Peter uses it twice: believers are to supply virtue to their faith generously (v. 5), and God will supply their entrance into the kingdom generously (v. 11). Peter used the simpler χορηγέω in 1 Peter 4:11 for the strength God supplies. Generosity in growth meets generosity in welcome."},{"english":"Self-control","hebrew":"ἐγκράτεια","translit":"enkráteia","strongs":"G1466 (perseverance ὑπομονή, hypomonḗ, G5281)","verses":"v. 6","insight":"Strong's: “self-control (especially continence).” ἐγκράτεια means mastery over oneself — holding power over one's own desires. It belongs to the self-control family, alongside σωφροσύνη (sound-mindedness) and νήφω (sobriety). Paul lists it as part of the fruit of the Spirit (Galatians 5:23). In 2 Peter it stands in sharp contrast to the false teachers of chapter 2, who promise freedom while being slaves to their desires. True knowledge produces self-control; false knowledge excuses its absence."},{"english":"Departure","hebrew":"ἔξοδος","translit":"éxodos","strongs":"G1841 (tent σκήνωμα, skḗnōma, G4638)","verses":"v. 15","insight":"Strong's: “an exit, i.e. (figuratively) death.” ἔξοδος is the word from which ‘exodus’ comes. It is used of Israel's departure from Egypt (Hebrews 11:22) and of Jesus' death in Luke 9:31, spoken of by Moses and Elijah on the mountain. Peter uses it of his own death. For the believer, death is not an ending but an exodus — a departure from slavery and a journey home. Peter pairs it with σκήνωμα, a tent: the body is a temporary dwelling, folded up when the journey moves on."},{"english":"Moved / carried along","hebrew":"φέρω","translit":"phérō","strongs":"G5342 (interpretation ἐπίλυσις, epílysis, G1955)","verses":"v. 17, 18, 21","insight":"Strong's: “to \"bear\" or carry (in a very wide application, literally and figuratively, as follows)”. Peter uses φέρω four times: the voice was ‘borne’ to Christ (v. 17) and ‘borne’ from heaven (v. 18); prophecy was never ‘borne’ by human will, but men spoke as they were ‘borne along’ by the Holy Spirit (v. 21). Luke uses it of a ship driven by the wind (Acts 27:15, 17). The prophets were not passive, but the Spirit was the wind in their sails. That is why their word is sure."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"In this chapter the Holy Spirit reminds you of what you already have, calls you to grow, and anchors you in a word you can trust.","items":[{"title":"You Are Not Lacking","body":"Do you feel that you need some special experience or secret before you can live a godly life? The Spirit says you already have “all things that pertain to life and godliness” (v. 3) in knowing Christ. What you need is not more supply but more diligence. Which of the qualities in verses 5–7 is He asking you to add right now?"},{"title":"Have You Become Short-Sighted?","body":"Peter warns of believers who are “blind, seeing only what is near, having forgotten the cleansing from his old sins” (v. 9). Are you so focused on today's problems and pleasures that you have lost sight of eternity? Have you forgotten how much you were forgiven? Ask the Spirit to clear your vision and remind you of the cross."},{"title":"Is the Lamp Lit?","body":"God has given you a lamp for the dark places — His word (v. 19). Are you walking by it, or stumbling in the dark? The Spirit who carried the prophets along wants to speak to you through what they wrote. Commit to heeding it daily until the day dawns and the morning star rises in your heart."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Growth Check","body":"Look at the list in verses 5–7. Which quality is strongest in you? Which is weakest? What is one step you can take to add the weakest one this month?"},{"title":"The Memory Check","body":"When did you last thank God specifically for the cleansing of your old sins (v. 9)? Have you grown proud, cold or careless because you have forgotten?"},{"title":"The Word Check","body":"Is Scripture your lamp, or only a decoration (v. 19)? Which voices — teachers, influencers, opinions — are you trusting more than God's word?"}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Eight Weeks Up the Ladder","body":"For the next eight weeks, focus on one quality from verses 5–7 each week: faith, moral excellence, knowledge, self-control, perseverance, godliness, brotherly affection, love. Each Monday, read the verse, find one other Scripture about that quality, and choose one practical way to practise it that week. Review at the end of each week."},{"title":"Leave a Reminder","body":"Peter wanted believers to remember after his departure (v. 15). Write a letter to someone younger in the faith — a child, a new believer, a friend — reminding them of the core truths of the gospel and the things you most want them to remember about following Jesus. Give it to them, or keep it to give at the right time."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Lord Jesus, our God and Saviour, thank You for giving me a faith as precious as the apostles' own. Multiply grace and peace to me as I grow in knowing You. Thank You that Your divine power has given me everything I need for life and godliness, and that through Your precious and very great promises I share in Your nature and escape the corruption of the world. Make me diligent. Add to my faith virtue, to virtue knowledge, to knowledge self-control, to self-control perseverance, to perseverance godliness, to godliness brotherly affection, and to brotherly affection love. Keep me from being idle or fruitless. Open my eyes where I have become short-sighted, and never let me forget that You cleansed me from my old sins. Confirm my calling and election in the way I live, and grant me a rich welcome into Your eternal kingdom. Thank You for eyewitnesses who saw Your majesty and heard the Father's voice, and for the prophetic word, spoken by men carried along by the Holy Spirit. Let Your word be my lamp in every dark place, until the day dawns and the morning star rises in my heart. When the time comes for my own tent to be folded, let my departure be an exodus home to You. In Your name, Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"The Transfiguration","ref":"Matthew 17:1-8; Luke 9:28-36","relatedVerses":"v. 16-18","summary":"Jesus took Peter, James and John up a high mountain to pray. As He prayed, His face shone like the sun and His clothes became dazzling white. Moses and Elijah appeared, speaking with Him about the departure He would accomplish at Jerusalem. Peter, half-awake and terrified, offered to build three shelters. Then a bright cloud overshadowed them, and a voice said, “This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased. Listen to him” (Matthew 17:5). The disciples fell on their faces until Jesus touched them. Decades later Peter writes that this was no myth: he was an eyewitness of Christ's majesty."},{"title":"Jesus Foretells Peter's Death","ref":"John 21:18-23","relatedVerses":"v. 14","summary":"After restoring Peter by the lake, Jesus said to him, “But when you are old, you will stretch out your hands, and another will dress you, and carry you where you don’t want to go” (John 21:18). “Now he said this, signifying by what kind of death he would glorify God. When he had said this, he said to him, ‘Follow me’” (John 21:19). Peter carried that word for more than thirty years. In 2 Peter he writes that the putting off of his tent is coming soon, ‘even as our Lord Jesus Christ made clear to me’. Church tradition says he was crucified in Rome under Nero."},{"title":"Paul's Ship Driven by the Wind","ref":"Acts 27:13-26","relatedVerses":"v. 21","summary":"On his voyage to Rome as a prisoner, Paul's ship was caught by a violent north-easterly wind. The sailors could not steer against it, “we gave way to it, and were driven along” (Acts 27:15). Luke uses the same verb, φέρω, that Peter uses for the prophets ‘carried along’ by the Holy Spirit. The ship was real, its crew was real, its timbers creaked — but its course was set by a power beyond them. So with the prophets: real men, real words, but carried along by the Spirit to speak from God."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"2 Peter","chapter":2,"chapterTitle":"False Teachers and the God Who Knows How to Rescue","themeTitle":"“The Lord Knows How to Deliver the Godly Out of Temptation”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"But false prophets also arose among the people, as false teachers will also be among you, who will secretly bring in destructive heresies, denying even the Master who bought them, bringing on themselves swift destruction.","understanding":"ἐγένοντο δὲ καὶ ψευδοπροφῆται ἐν τῷ λαῷ, “But false prophets also arose among the people” — ψευδοπροφήτης, contrasting with the true prophets of 1:19–21. ὡς καὶ ἐν ὑμῖν ἔσονται ψευδοδιδάσκαλοι, “as false teachers will also be among you” — inside the church. οἵτινες παρεισάξουσιν αἱρέσεις ἀπωλείας, “who will secretly bring in destructive heresies” — παρεισάγω, to smuggle in alongside; αἵρεσις, a chosen opinion, a faction; ἀπώλεια, ruin. καὶ τὸν ἀγοράσαντα αὐτοὺς δεσπότην ἀρνούμενοι, “denying even the Master who bought them” — δεσπότης, the owner of slaves; ἀγοράζω, from the redeem family: to buy in the market-place. They deny with their lives the Lord whose purchase they claimed. Christians differ on how to read ‘bought’ here — whether Christ's death was for those who finally perish, or whether they only professed to be redeemed — but the verse's force is clear: they are traitors to the Master they named. ἐπάγοντες ἑαυτοῖς ταχινὴν ἀπώλειαν, “bringing on themselves swift destruction.”","words":[{"strongs":"G5578","hebrew":"ψευδοπροφήτης","translit":"pseudoprophḗtēs","english":"false prophets"},{"strongs":"G2992","hebrew":"λαός","translit":"laós","english":"people"},{"strongs":"G5572","hebrew":"ψευδοδιδάσκαλος","translit":"pseudodidáskalos","english":"false teachers"},{"strongs":"G3919","hebrew":"παρεισάγω","translit":"pareiságō","english":"secretly bring in"},{"strongs":"G684","hebrew":"ἀπώλεια","translit":"apṓleia","english":"destructive"},{"strongs":"G139","hebrew":"αἵρεσις","translit":"haíresis","english":"heresies"},{"strongs":"G59","hebrew":"ἀγοράζω","translit":"agorázō","english":"bought"},{"strongs":"G1203","hebrew":"δεσπότης","translit":"despótēs","english":"Master"},{"strongs":"G720","hebrew":"ἀρνέομαι","translit":"arnéomai","english":"denying"},{"strongs":"G1863","hebrew":"ἐπάγω","translit":"epágō","english":"bringing"},{"strongs":"G5031","hebrew":"ταχινός","translit":"tachinós","english":"swift"}]},{"v":2,"text":"Many will follow their immoral ways, and as a result, the way of the truth will be maligned.","understanding":"καὶ πολλοὶ ἐξακολουθήσουσιν αὐτῶν ταῖς ἀσελγείαις, “Many will follow their immoral ways” — ἐξακολουθέω, the ‘follow’ of 1:16, where the apostles did not follow fables; here people follow sensuality. ἀσέλγεια, shameless sexual excess. δι᾽ οὓς ἡ ὁδὸς τῆς ἀληθείας βλασφημηθήσεται, “and as a result, the way of the truth will be maligned” — ὁδός, the Way, an early name for Christianity (Acts 9:2); βλασφημέω, to slander. Immoral teachers bring disgrace on Christ's name.","words":[{"strongs":"G1811","hebrew":"ἐξακολουθέω","translit":"exakolouthéō","english":"follow"},{"strongs":"G766","hebrew":"ἀσέλγεια","translit":"asélgeia","english":"immoral ways"},{"strongs":"G3598","hebrew":"ὁδός","translit":"hodós","english":"way"},{"strongs":"G225","hebrew":"ἀλήθεια","translit":"alḗtheia","english":"truth"},{"strongs":"G987","hebrew":"βλασφημέω","translit":"blasphēméō","english":"maligned"}]},{"v":3,"text":"In covetousness they will exploit you with deceptive words: whose sentence now from of old doesn’t linger, and their destruction will not slumber.","understanding":"καὶ ἐν πλεονεξίᾳ πλαστοῖς λόγοις ὑμᾶς ἐμπορεύσονται, “In covetousness they will exploit you with deceptive words” — πλεονεξία, greed, wanting more; πλαστός, moulded, fabricated (our word ‘plastic’); λόγος, from the word family: their words are manufactured. ἐμπορεύομαι, to trade in, the merchant's word of James 4:13: they treat believers as merchandise. οἷς τὸ κρίμα ἔκπαλαι οὐκ ἀργεῖ, “whose sentence now from of old doesn’t linger” — ἀργέω, to be idle: their judgement is not idle. καὶ ἡ ἀπώλεια αὐτῶν οὐ νυστάζει, “and their destruction will not slumber” — νυστάζω, from the sleep family: to nod off. Judgement is awake.","words":[{"strongs":"G4124","hebrew":"πλεονεξία","translit":"pleonexía","english":"covetousness"},{"strongs":"G4112","hebrew":"πλαστός","translit":"plastós","english":"deceptive"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"words"},{"strongs":"G1710","hebrew":"ἐμπορεύομαι","translit":"emporeúomai","english":"exploit"},{"strongs":"G2917","hebrew":"κρίμα","translit":"kríma","english":"sentence"},{"strongs":"G1597","hebrew":"ἔκπαλαι","translit":"ékpalai","english":"from of old"},{"strongs":"G691","hebrew":"ἀργέω","translit":"argéō","english":"linger"},{"strongs":"G684","hebrew":"ἀπώλεια","translit":"apṓleia","english":"destruction"},{"strongs":"G3573","hebrew":"νυστάζω","translit":"nystázō","english":"slumber"}]},{"v":4,"text":"For if God didn’t spare angels when they sinned, but cast them down to Tartarus, and committed them to pits of darkness, to be reserved for judgment;","understanding":"εἰ γὰρ ὁ θεὸς ἀγγέλων ἁμαρτησάντων οὐκ ἐφείσατο, “For if God didn’t spare angels when they sinned” — ἁμαρτάνω, from the sin family. Three examples of judgement follow, each with a rescue. ἀλλὰ σειραῖς ζόφου ταρταρώσας, “but cast them down to Tartarus” — ταρταρόω, to consign to Tartarus, the Greek name for the deepest pit of the underworld; Peter borrows it for the prison of fallen angels (Jude 6). σειραῖς or σιροῖς — manuscripts vary between ‘chains’ and ‘pits’; the WEB reads ‘pits of darkness’, ζόφος, thick gloom. παρέδωκεν εἰς κρίσιν τηρουμένους, “and committed them … to be reserved for judgment” — τηρέω, from the hold family: kept under guard.","words":[{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angels"},{"strongs":"G264","hebrew":"ἁμαρτάνω","translit":"hamartánō","english":"sinned"},{"strongs":"G5339","hebrew":"φείδομαι","translit":"pheídomai","english":"spare"},{"strongs":"G5020","hebrew":"ταρταρόω","translit":"tartaróō","english":"cast them down to Tartarus"},{"strongs":"G3860","hebrew":"παραδίδωμι","translit":"paradídōmi","english":"committed"},{"strongs":"G4577","hebrew":"σειρά","translit":"seirá","english":"pits"},{"strongs":"G2217","hebrew":"ζόφος","translit":"zóphos","english":"darkness"},{"strongs":"G5083","hebrew":"τηρέω","translit":"tēréō","english":"reserved"},{"strongs":"G2920","hebrew":"κρίσις","translit":"krísis","english":"judgment"}]},{"v":5,"text":"and didn’t spare the ancient world, but preserved Noah with seven others, a preacher of righteousness, when he brought a flood on the world of the ungodly;","understanding":"καὶ ἀρχαίου κόσμου οὐκ ἐφείσατο, “and didn’t spare the ancient world” — κόσμος, from the world family: the inhabited world of Noah's day. ἀλλὰ ὄγδοον Νῶε δικαιοσύνης κήρυκα ἐφύλαξεν, “but preserved Noah with seven others, a preacher of righteousness” — literally ‘Noah the eighth’; φυλάσσω, also from the hold family: to guard, as distinct from τηρέω in verse 4 — the angels are kept for judgement, Noah is guarded for life. κῆρυξ, a herald: Noah warned his generation. κατακλυσμὸν κόσμῳ ἀσεβῶν ἐπάξας, “when he brought a flood on the world of the ungodly” — ἀσεβής, from the godliness family.","words":[{"strongs":"G744","hebrew":"ἀρχαῖος","translit":"archaîos","english":"ancient"},{"strongs":"G2889","hebrew":"κόσμος","translit":"kósmos","english":"world"},{"strongs":"G5339","hebrew":"φείδομαι","translit":"pheídomai","english":"spare"},{"strongs":"G5442","hebrew":"φυλάσσω","translit":"phylássō","english":"preserved"},{"strongs":"G3575","hebrew":"Νῶε","translit":"Nōe","english":"Noah"},{"strongs":"G3590","hebrew":"ὄγδοος","translit":"ógdoos","english":"with seven others"},{"strongs":"G2783","hebrew":"κῆρυξ","translit":"kēryx","english":"preacher"},{"strongs":"G1343","hebrew":"δικαιοσύνη","translit":"dikaiosýnē","english":"righteousness"},{"strongs":"G2627","hebrew":"κατακλυσμός","translit":"kataklysmós","english":"flood"},{"strongs":"G1863","hebrew":"ἐπάγω","translit":"epágō","english":"brought"},{"strongs":"G765","hebrew":"ἀσεβής","translit":"asebḗs","english":"ungodly"}]},{"v":6,"text":"and turning the cities of Sodom and Gomorrah into ashes, condemned them to destruction, having made them an example to those who would live in an ungodly way;","understanding":"καὶ πόλεις Σοδόμων καὶ Γομόρρας τεφρώσας, “and turning the cities of Sodom and Gomorrah into ashes” — τεφρόω, to reduce to ash. καταστροφῇ κατέκρινεν, “condemned them to destruction” — κατακρίνω, to pass sentence. ὑπόδειγμα μελλόντων ἀσεβεῖν τεθεικώς, “having made them an example to those who would live in an ungodly way” — ὑπόδειγμα, from the pattern family: an example set up as a warning; ἀσεβέω, from the godliness family: to act without reverence for God. Jesus used the same examples (Luke 17:28–29).","words":[{"strongs":"G4172","hebrew":"πόλις","translit":"pólis","english":"cities"},{"strongs":"G4670","hebrew":"Σόδομα","translit":"Sódoma","english":"Sodom"},{"strongs":"G1116","hebrew":"Γόμοῤῥα","translit":"Gómorrha","english":"Gomorrah"},{"strongs":"G5077","hebrew":"τεφρόω","translit":"tephróō","english":"into ashes"},{"strongs":"G2632","hebrew":"κατακρίνω","translit":"katakrínō","english":"condemned"},{"strongs":"G2692","hebrew":"καταστροφή","translit":"katastrophḗ","english":"destruction"},{"strongs":"G5262","hebrew":"ὑπόδειγμα","translit":"hypódeigma","english":"example"},{"strongs":"G764","hebrew":"ἀσεβέω","translit":"asebéō","english":"ungodly"}]},{"v":7,"text":"and delivered righteous Lot, who was very distressed by the lustful life of the wicked","understanding":"καὶ δίκαιον Λὼτ καταπονούμενον, “and delivered righteous Lot, who was very distressed” — καταπονέω, worn down, oppressed. ὑπὸ τῆς τῶν ἀθέσμων ἐν ἀσελγείᾳ ἀναστροφῆς, “by the lustful life of the wicked” — ἄθεσμος, lawless, without any moral boundary; ἀσέλγεια, as in verse 2. ἐρρύσατο, “delivered” — ῥύομαι, to rescue, snatch out of danger. Genesis shows Lot's weakness; Peter shows that God still counted him righteous and rescued him.","words":[{"strongs":"G1342","hebrew":"δίκαιος","translit":"díkaios","english":"righteous"},{"strongs":"G3091","hebrew":"Λώτ","translit":"Lṓt","english":"Lot"},{"strongs":"G2669","hebrew":"καταπονέω","translit":"kataponéō","english":"very distressed"},{"strongs":"G113","hebrew":"ἄθεσμος","translit":"áthesmos","english":"wicked"},{"strongs":"G766","hebrew":"ἀσέλγεια","translit":"asélgeia","english":"lustful"},{"strongs":"G391","hebrew":"ἀναστροφή","translit":"anastrophḗ","english":"life"},{"strongs":"G4506","hebrew":"ῥύομαι","translit":"rhýomai","english":"delivered"}]},{"v":8,"text":"(for that righteous man dwelling among them, was tormented in his righteous soul from day to day with seeing and hearing lawless deeds):","understanding":"“for that righteous man dwelling among them, was tormented in his righteous soul from day to day with seeing and hearing lawless deeds” — ἡμέρα ἐξ ἡμέρας, from the time family: day after day; βλέμμα, from the see family: what he saw; ἀκοή, what he heard; ἐγκατοικέω, to live among; ψυχή, from the life family; ἄνομος, from the sin family: lawless; ἔργον, from the work family; βασανίζω, to torture, to rack. Living in a corrupt culture tormented Lot's conscience daily. Grief over sin is a mark of the righteous.","words":[{"strongs":"G1342","hebrew":"δίκαιος","translit":"díkaios","english":"righteous"},{"strongs":"G1460","hebrew":"ἐγκατοικέω","translit":"enkatoikéō","english":"dwelling"},{"strongs":"G990","hebrew":"βλέμμα","translit":"blémma","english":"seeing"},{"strongs":"G189","hebrew":"ἀκοή","translit":"akoḗ","english":"hearing"},{"strongs":"G2250","hebrew":"ἡμέρα","translit":"hēméra","english":"day"},{"strongs":"G5590","hebrew":"ψυχή","translit":"psychḗ","english":"soul"},{"strongs":"G459","hebrew":"ἄνομος","translit":"ánomos","english":"lawless"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"deeds"},{"strongs":"G928","hebrew":"βασανίζω","translit":"basanízō","english":"tormented"}]},{"v":9,"text":"the Lord knows how to deliver the godly out of temptation and to keep the unrighteous under punishment for the day of judgment;","understanding":"οἶδεν κύριος εὐσεβεῖς ἐκ πειρασμοῦ ῥύεσθαι, “the Lord knows how to deliver the godly out of temptation” — εἴδω, from the know family (and the see family); εὐσεβής, from the godliness family: the devout; πειρασμός, from the test family: trial, testing, pressure; ῥύομαι, as in verse 7. ἀδίκους δὲ εἰς ἡμέραν κρίσεως κολαζομένους τηρεῖν, “and to keep the unrighteous under punishment for the day of judgment” — ἡμέρα, from the time family: the appointed day; τηρέω, from the hold family; κολάζω, to punish, to restrain. This is the point of the long sentence from verse 4: God rescues and God judges, and He knows how to do both.","words":[{"strongs":"G1492","hebrew":"εἴδω","translit":"eídō","english":"knows"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G4506","hebrew":"ῥύομαι","translit":"rhýomai","english":"deliver"},{"strongs":"G2152","hebrew":"εὐσεβής","translit":"eusebḗs","english":"godly"},{"strongs":"G3986","hebrew":"πειρασμός","translit":"peirasmós","english":"temptation"},{"strongs":"G94","hebrew":"ἄδικος","translit":"ádikos","english":"unrighteous"},{"strongs":"G2250","hebrew":"ἡμέρα","translit":"hēméra","english":"day"},{"strongs":"G2920","hebrew":"κρίσις","translit":"krísis","english":"judgment"},{"strongs":"G2849","hebrew":"κολάζω","translit":"kolázō","english":"punishment"},{"strongs":"G5083","hebrew":"τηρέω","translit":"tēréō","english":"keep"}]},{"v":10,"text":"but chiefly those who walk after the flesh in the lust of defilement, and despise authority. Daring, self-willed, they are not afraid to speak evil of dignitaries;","understanding":"μάλιστα δὲ τοὺς ὀπίσω σαρκὸς ἐν ἐπιθυμίᾳ μιασμοῦ πορευομένους, “but chiefly those who walk after the flesh in the lust of defilement” — σάρξ; ἐπιθυμία; μιασμός, pollution. καὶ κυριότητος καταφρονοῦντας, “and despise authority” — κυριότης, from the power family: lordship, dominion (Ephesians 1:21), perhaps the Lord's own authority. τολμηταὶ αὐθάδεις, “Daring, self-willed” — τολμητής, reckless; αὐθάδης, self-pleasing, arrogant. δόξας οὐ τρέμουσιν βλασφημοῦντες, “they are not afraid to speak evil of dignitaries” — δόξα, ‘glorious ones’, probably angelic beings; τρέμω, to tremble.","words":[{"strongs":"G4561","hebrew":"σάρξ","translit":"sárx","english":"flesh"},{"strongs":"G1939","hebrew":"ἐπιθυμία","translit":"epithymía","english":"lust"},{"strongs":"G3394","hebrew":"μιασμός","translit":"miasmós","english":"defilement"},{"strongs":"G4198","hebrew":"πορεύομαι","translit":"poreúomai","english":"walk"},{"strongs":"G2963","hebrew":"κυριότης","translit":"kyriótēs","english":"authority"},{"strongs":"G2706","hebrew":"καταφρονέω","translit":"kataphronéō","english":"despise"},{"strongs":"G5113","hebrew":"τολμητής","translit":"tolmētḗs","english":"Daring"},{"strongs":"G829","hebrew":"αὐθάδης","translit":"authádēs","english":"self-willed"},{"strongs":"G5141","hebrew":"τρέμω","translit":"trémō","english":"afraid"},{"strongs":"G1391","hebrew":"δόξα","translit":"dóxa","english":"dignitaries"},{"strongs":"G987","hebrew":"βλασφημέω","translit":"blasphēméō","english":"speak evil"}]},{"v":11,"text":"whereas angels, though greater in might and power, don’t bring a railing judgment against them before the Lord.","understanding":"ὅπου ἄγγελοι ἰσχύϊ καὶ δυνάμει μείζονες ὄντες, “whereas angels, though greater in might and power” — two words from the power family: ἰσχύς, inherent strength, and δύναμις, ability in action. οὐ φέρουσιν κατ᾽ αὐτῶν παρὰ κυρίῳ βλάσφημον κρίσιν, “don’t bring a railing judgment against them before the Lord” — βλάσφημος, abusive. Even mighty angels leave judgement to God (compare Jude 9). The false teachers have less power and less restraint.","words":[{"strongs":"G32","hebrew":"ἄγγελος","translit":"ángelos","english":"angels"},{"strongs":"G2479","hebrew":"ἰσχύς","translit":"ischýs","english":"might"},{"strongs":"G1411","hebrew":"δύναμις","translit":"dýnamis","english":"power"},{"strongs":"G3187","hebrew":"μείζων","translit":"meízōn","english":"greater"},{"strongs":"G5342","hebrew":"φέρω","translit":"phérō","english":"bring"},{"strongs":"G989","hebrew":"βλάσφημος","translit":"blásphēmos","english":"railing"},{"strongs":"G2920","hebrew":"κρίσις","translit":"krísis","english":"judgment"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"}]},{"v":12,"text":"But these, as unreasoning creatures, born natural animals to be taken and destroyed, speaking evil in matters about which they are ignorant, will in their destroying surely be destroyed,","understanding":"οὗτοι δέ, ὡς ἄλογα ζῷα, “But these, as unreasoning creatures” — ἄλογος, without reason, the opposite of λόγος; ζῷον, an animal. γεγεννημένα φυσικὰ εἰς ἅλωσιν καὶ φθοράν, “born natural animals to be taken and destroyed” — γεννάω, from the birth family; φυσικός, by nature; ἅλωσις, capture. ἐν οἷς ἀγνοοῦσιν βλασφημοῦντες, “speaking evil in matters about which they are ignorant” — ἀγνοέω, not to know. ἐν τῇ φθορᾷ αὐτῶν καταφθαρήσονται, “will in their destroying surely be destroyed” — φθορά, corruption, the decay the believers escaped (1:4). They live like beasts and will die like them.","words":[{"strongs":"G249","hebrew":"ἄλογος","translit":"álogos","english":"unreasoning"},{"strongs":"G2226","hebrew":"ζῶον","translit":"zōon","english":"creatures"},{"strongs":"G1080","hebrew":"γεννάω","translit":"gennáō","english":"born"},{"strongs":"G5446","hebrew":"φυσικός","translit":"physikós","english":"natural"},{"strongs":"G259","hebrew":"ἅλωσις","translit":"hálōsis","english":"taken"},{"strongs":"G5356","hebrew":"φθορά","translit":"phthorá","english":"destroyed"},{"strongs":"G50","hebrew":"ἀγνοέω","translit":"agnoéō","english":"ignorant"},{"strongs":"G987","hebrew":"βλασφημέω","translit":"blasphēméō","english":"speaking evil"},{"strongs":"G2704","hebrew":"καταφθείρω","translit":"kataphtheírō","english":"surely be destroyed"}]},{"v":13,"text":"receiving the wages of unrighteousness; people who count it pleasure to revel in the daytime, spots and defects, reveling in their deceit while they feast with you;","understanding":"κομιούμενοι μισθὸν ἀδικίας, “receiving the wages of unrighteousness” — μισθός, pay; ἀδικία, from the sin family: wrongdoing. ἡδονὴν ἡγούμενοι τὴν ἐν ἡμέρᾳ τρυφήν, “people who count it pleasure to revel in the daytime” — ἡδονή, the same ‘pleasures’ James says wage war in the members; τρυφή, luxurious living; ἡμέρα, from the time family: even pagans waited till night. σπίλοι καὶ μῶμοι, “spots and defects” — the exact opposite of Christ, the Lamb ‘without blemish or spot’ (1 Peter 1:19). ἐντρυφῶντες ἐν ταῖς ἀπάταις αὐτῶν, “reveling in their deceit” — ἀπάτη, deceit; some manuscripts read ἀγάπαις, ‘love feasts’ (Jude 12). συνευωχούμενοι ὑμῖν, “while they feast with you” — at the church's fellowship meals.","words":[{"strongs":"G2865","hebrew":"κομίζω","translit":"komízō","english":"receiving"},{"strongs":"G3408","hebrew":"μισθός","translit":"misthós","english":"wages"},{"strongs":"G93","hebrew":"ἀδικία","translit":"adikía","english":"unrighteousness"},{"strongs":"G2237","hebrew":"ἡδονή","translit":"hēdonḗ","english":"pleasure"},{"strongs":"G2233","hebrew":"ἡγέομαι","translit":"hēgéomai","english":"count"},{"strongs":"G5172","hebrew":"τρυφή","translit":"tryphḗ","english":"revel"},{"strongs":"G2250","hebrew":"ἡμέρα","translit":"hēméra","english":"daytime"},{"strongs":"G4696","hebrew":"σπίλος","translit":"spílos","english":"spots"},{"strongs":"G3470","hebrew":"μῶμος","translit":"mōmos","english":"defects"},{"strongs":"G1792","hebrew":"ἐντρυφάω","translit":"entrypháō","english":"reveling"},{"strongs":"G539","hebrew":"ἀπάτη","translit":"apátē","english":"deceit"},{"strongs":"G4910","hebrew":"συνευωχέω","translit":"syneuōchéō","english":"feast with"}]},{"v":14,"text":"having eyes full of adultery, and who can’t cease from sin; enticing unsettled souls; having a heart trained in greed; children of cursing;","understanding":"ὀφθαλμοὺς ἔχοντες μεστοὺς μοιχαλίδος, “having eyes full of adultery” — every woman is seen as a possible conquest. καὶ ἀκαταπαύστους ἁμαρτίας, “and who can’t cease from sin” — ἀκατάπαυστος, unceasing; ἁμαρτία, from the sin family. δελεάζοντες ψυχὰς ἀστηρίκτους, “enticing unsettled souls” — δελεάζω, to bait a hook, the word of James 1:14; ἀστήρικτος, unstable, the opposite of the believers ‘established’ (στηρίζω) in 1:12; ψυχή, from the life family. καρδίαν γεγυμνασμένην πλεονεξίας ἔχοντες, “having a heart trained in greed” — γυμνάζω, from the discipline family: to train like an athlete. They are disciplined — in sin. κατάρας τέκνα, “children of cursing.”","words":[{"strongs":"G3788","hebrew":"ὀφθαλμός","translit":"ophthalmós","english":"eyes"},{"strongs":"G3324","hebrew":"μεστός","translit":"mestós","english":"full"},{"strongs":"G3428","hebrew":"μοιχαλίς","translit":"moichalís","english":"adultery"},{"strongs":"G180","hebrew":"ἀκατάπαυστος","translit":"akatápaustos","english":"can’t cease"},{"strongs":"G266","hebrew":"ἁμαρτία","translit":"hamartía","english":"sin"},{"strongs":"G1185","hebrew":"δελεάζω","translit":"deleázō","english":"enticing"},{"strongs":"G793","hebrew":"ἀστήρικτος","translit":"astḗriktos","english":"unsettled"},{"strongs":"G5590","hebrew":"ψυχή","translit":"psychḗ","english":"souls"},{"strongs":"G2588","hebrew":"καρδία","translit":"kardía","english":"heart"},{"strongs":"G1128","hebrew":"γυμνάζω","translit":"gymnázō","english":"trained"},{"strongs":"G4124","hebrew":"πλεονεξία","translit":"pleonexía","english":"greed"},{"strongs":"G2671","hebrew":"κατάρα","translit":"katára","english":"cursing"},{"strongs":"G5043","hebrew":"τέκνον","translit":"téknon","english":"children"}]},{"v":15,"text":"forsaking the right way, they went astray, having followed the way of Balaam the son of Beor, who loved the wages of wrongdoing;","understanding":"καταλιπόντες εὐθεῖαν ὁδόν, “forsaking the right way” — καταλείπω, to abandon; εὐθύς, straight. ἐπλανήθησαν, “they went astray” — πλανάω, to wander off, the word James uses for the believer who wanders from the truth. ἐξακολουθήσαντες τῇ ὁδῷ τοῦ Βαλαὰμ τοῦ Βοσόρ, “having followed the way of Balaam the son of Beor” — the prophet hired to curse Israel (Numbers 22–24), who then taught Moab to lead Israel into idolatry and immorality (Numbers 31:16; Revelation 2:14). ὃς μισθὸν ἀδικίας ἠγάπησεν, “who loved the wages of wrongdoing” — ἀγαπάω, from the love family, here turned to a wrong object; ἀδικία, from the sin family, as in verse 13. Greed for gain under religious cover.","words":[{"strongs":"G2641","hebrew":"καταλείπω","translit":"kataleípō","english":"forsaking"},{"strongs":"G2117","hebrew":"εὐθύς","translit":"euthýs","english":"right"},{"strongs":"G3598","hebrew":"ὁδός","translit":"hodós","english":"way"},{"strongs":"G4105","hebrew":"πλανάω","translit":"planáō","english":"went astray"},{"strongs":"G1811","hebrew":"ἐξακολουθέω","translit":"exakolouthéō","english":"followed"},{"strongs":"G903","hebrew":"Βαλαάμ","translit":"Balaám","english":"Balaam"},{"strongs":"G1007","hebrew":"Βοσόρ","translit":"Bosór","english":"Beor"},{"strongs":"G3408","hebrew":"μισθός","translit":"misthós","english":"wages"},{"strongs":"G93","hebrew":"ἀδικία","translit":"adikía","english":"wrongdoing"},{"strongs":"G25","hebrew":"ἀγαπάω","translit":"agapáō","english":"loved"}]},{"v":16,"text":"but he was rebuked for his own disobedience. A mute donkey spoke with a man’s voice and stopped the madness of the prophet.","understanding":"ἔλεγξιν δὲ ἔσχεν ἰδίας παρανομίας, “but he was rebuked for his own disobedience” — ἔλεγξις, from the discipline family: exposure, rebuke; παρανομία, from the sin family: acting against the law. ὑποζύγιον ἄφωνον ἐν ἀνθρώπου φωνῇ φθεγξάμενον, “A mute donkey spoke with a man’s voice” — ὑποζύγιον, a beast of burden; ἄφωνος, voiceless; φθέγγομαι, to utter. ἐκώλυσεν τὴν τοῦ προφήτου παραφρονίαν, “and stopped the madness of the prophet” — παραφρονία, insanity. The irony: a dumb animal saw what the greedy prophet could not, and spoke more sense than he did.","words":[{"strongs":"G1649","hebrew":"ἔλεγξις","translit":"élenxis","english":"rebuked"},{"strongs":"G3892","hebrew":"παρανομία","translit":"paranomía","english":"disobedience"},{"strongs":"G880","hebrew":"ἄφωνος","translit":"áphōnos","english":"mute"},{"strongs":"G5268","hebrew":"ὑποζύγιον","translit":"hypozýgion","english":"donkey"},{"strongs":"G5350","hebrew":"φθέγγομαι","translit":"phthéngomai","english":"spoke"},{"strongs":"G444","hebrew":"ἄνθρωπος","translit":"ánthrōpos","english":"man’s"},{"strongs":"G5456","hebrew":"φωνή","translit":"phōnḗ","english":"voice"},{"strongs":"G2967","hebrew":"κωλύω","translit":"kōlýō","english":"stopped"},{"strongs":"G3913","hebrew":"παραφρονία","translit":"paraphronía","english":"madness"},{"strongs":"G4396","hebrew":"προφήτης","translit":"prophḗtēs","english":"prophet"}]},{"v":17,"text":"These are wells without water, clouds driven by a storm; for whom the blackness of darkness has been reserved forever.","understanding":"οὗτοί εἰσιν πηγαὶ ἄνυδροι, “These are wells without water” — πηγή, a spring, the ‘spring’ of James 3:11; ἄνυδρος, dry: they promise refreshment and give none. νεφέλαι ὑπὸ λαίλαπος ἐλαυνόμεναι, “clouds driven by a storm” — clouds that promise rain to a thirsty farmer but are blown away. ἐλαύνω, the ‘driven’ of James 3:4. οἷς ὁ ζόφος τοῦ σκότους τετήρηται, “for whom the blackness of darkness has been reserved forever” — ζόφος, the gloom of verse 4; τηρέω, from the hold family, as for the angels; αἰών, in the time family and the world family.","words":[{"strongs":"G4077","hebrew":"πηγή","translit":"pēgḗ","english":"wells"},{"strongs":"G504","hebrew":"ἄνυδρος","translit":"ánydros","english":"without water"},{"strongs":"G3507","hebrew":"νεφέλη","translit":"nephélē","english":"clouds"},{"strongs":"G2978","hebrew":"λαῖλαψ","translit":"laîlaps","english":"storm"},{"strongs":"G1643","hebrew":"ἐλαύνω","translit":"elaúnō","english":"driven"},{"strongs":"G2217","hebrew":"ζόφος","translit":"zóphos","english":"blackness"},{"strongs":"G4655","hebrew":"σκότος","translit":"skótos","english":"darkness"},{"strongs":"G5083","hebrew":"τηρέω","translit":"tēréō","english":"reserved"},{"strongs":"G165","hebrew":"αἰών","translit":"aiṓn","english":"forever"}]},{"v":18,"text":"For, uttering great swelling words of emptiness, they entice in the lusts of the flesh, by licentiousness, those who are indeed escaping from those who live in error;","understanding":"ὑπέρογκα γὰρ ματαιότητος φθεγγόμενοι, “For, uttering great swelling words of emptiness” — ὑπέρογκος, inflated, bombastic; ματαιότης, emptiness, futility. They sound impressive and say nothing. δελεάζουσιν ἐν ἐπιθυμίαις σαρκὸς ἀσελγείαις, “they entice in the lusts of the flesh, by licentiousness” — δελεάζω, as in verse 14. τοὺς ὄντως ἀποφεύγοντας τοὺς ἐν πλάνῃ ἀναστρεφομένους, “those who are indeed escaping from those who live in error” — new believers just leaving paganism are the most vulnerable; πλάνη, the ‘error’ of James 5:20.","words":[{"strongs":"G5246","hebrew":"ὑπέρογκος","translit":"hypéronkos","english":"great swelling"},{"strongs":"G3153","hebrew":"ματαιότης","translit":"mataiótēs","english":"emptiness"},{"strongs":"G5350","hebrew":"φθέγγομαι","translit":"phthéngomai","english":"uttering"},{"strongs":"G1185","hebrew":"δελεάζω","translit":"deleázō","english":"entice"},{"strongs":"G1939","hebrew":"ἐπιθυμία","translit":"epithymía","english":"lusts"},{"strongs":"G4561","hebrew":"σάρξ","translit":"sárx","english":"flesh"},{"strongs":"G766","hebrew":"ἀσέλγεια","translit":"asélgeia","english":"licentiousness"},{"strongs":"G668","hebrew":"ἀποφεύγω","translit":"apopheúgō","english":"escaping"},{"strongs":"G4106","hebrew":"πλάνη","translit":"plánē","english":"error"},{"strongs":"G390","hebrew":"ἀναστρέφω","translit":"anastréphō","english":"live"}]},{"v":19,"text":"promising them liberty, while they themselves are bondservants of corruption; for a man is brought into bondage by whoever overcomes him.","understanding":"ἐλευθερίαν αὐτοῖς ἐπαγγελλόμενοι, “promising them liberty” — ἐλευθερία, the word James uses for ‘the perfect law of freedom’, here falsely offered as freedom from moral restraint. αὐτοὶ δοῦλοι ὑπάρχοντες τῆς φθορᾶς, “while they themselves are bondservants of corruption” — δοῦλος, from the servant family. ᾧ γάρ τις ἥττηται, τούτῳ δεδούλωται, “for a man is brought into bondage by whoever overcomes him” — ἡττάω, to be defeated; δουλόω, also from the servant family: to enslave. What you cannot say no to owns you (John 8:34).","words":[{"strongs":"G1657","hebrew":"ἐλευθερία","translit":"eleuthería","english":"liberty"},{"strongs":"G1861","hebrew":"ἐπαγγέλλω","translit":"epangéllō","english":"promising"},{"strongs":"G1401","hebrew":"δοῦλος","translit":"doûlos","english":"bondservants"},{"strongs":"G5356","hebrew":"φθορά","translit":"phthorá","english":"corruption"},{"strongs":"G2274","hebrew":"ἡττάω","translit":"hēttáō","english":"overcomes"},{"strongs":"G1402","hebrew":"δουλόω","translit":"doulóō","english":"brought into bondage"}]},{"v":20,"text":"For if, after they have escaped the defilement of the world through the knowledge of the Lord and Savior Jesus Christ, they are again entangled in it and overcome, the last state has become worse for them than the first.","understanding":"εἰ γὰρ ἀποφυγόντες τὰ μιάσματα τοῦ κόσμου, “For if, after they have escaped the defilement of the world” — ἀποφεύγω, the ‘escaped’ of 1:4; μίασμα, stain; κόσμος, from the world family. ἐν ἐπιγνώσει τοῦ κυρίου καὶ σωτῆρος Ἰησοῦ Χριστοῦ, “through the knowledge of the Lord and Savior Jesus Christ” — ἐπίγνωσις, from the know family, as in 1:2–3. τούτοις δὲ πάλιν ἐμπλακέντες ἡττῶνται, “they are again entangled in it and overcome” — ἐμπλέκω, to weave in, entangle. γέγονεν αὐτοῖς τὰ ἔσχατα χείρονα τῶν πρώτων, “the last state has become worse for them than the first” — almost exactly the words Jesus used of the man whose evil spirit returned with seven others.","words":[{"strongs":"G668","hebrew":"ἀποφεύγω","translit":"apopheúgō","english":"escaped"},{"strongs":"G3393","hebrew":"μίασμα","translit":"míasma","english":"defilement"},{"strongs":"G2889","hebrew":"κόσμος","translit":"kósmos","english":"world"},{"strongs":"G1922","hebrew":"ἐπίγνωσις","translit":"epígnōsis","english":"knowledge"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G4990","hebrew":"σωτήρ","translit":"sōtḗr","english":"Savior"},{"strongs":"G1707","hebrew":"ἐμπλέκω","translit":"emplékō","english":"entangled"},{"strongs":"G2274","hebrew":"ἡττάω","translit":"hēttáō","english":"overcome"},{"strongs":"G2078","hebrew":"ἔσχατος","translit":"éschatos","english":"last"},{"strongs":"G5501","hebrew":"χείρων","translit":"cheírōn","english":"worse"},{"strongs":"G4413","hebrew":"πρῶτος","translit":"prōtos","english":"first"}]},{"v":21,"text":"For it would be better for them not to have known the way of righteousness, than, after knowing it, to turn back from the holy commandment delivered to them.","understanding":"κρεῖττον γὰρ ἦν αὐτοῖς μὴ ἐπεγνωκέναι τὴν ὁδὸν τῆς δικαιοσύνης, “For it would be better for them not to have known the way of righteousness” — κρείττων, better; ἐπιγινώσκω, from the know family, the verb of ἐπίγνωσις. ἢ ἐπιγνοῦσιν ὑποστρέψαι ἐκ τῆς παραδοθείσης αὐτοῖς ἁγίας ἐντολῆς, “than, after knowing it, to turn back from the holy commandment delivered to them” — ἐντολή, the commandment; παραδίδωμι, handed down. Knowledge brings responsibility. Whether these were genuine believers who fell away or people who only tasted the truth without new birth is debated (compare Hebrews 6:4–6); verse 22 suggests their nature was never changed.","words":[{"strongs":"G2909","hebrew":"κρείττων","translit":"kreíttōn","english":"better"},{"strongs":"G1921","hebrew":"ἐπιγινώσκω","translit":"epiginṓskō","english":"known"},{"strongs":"G3598","hebrew":"ὁδός","translit":"hodós","english":"way"},{"strongs":"G1343","hebrew":"δικαιοσύνη","translit":"dikaiosýnē","english":"righteousness"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"holy"},{"strongs":"G1785","hebrew":"ἐντολή","translit":"entolḗ","english":"commandment"},{"strongs":"G3860","hebrew":"παραδίδωμι","translit":"paradídōmi","english":"delivered"}]},{"v":22,"text":"But it has happened to them according to the true proverb, “The dog turns to his own vomit again,” and “the sow that has washed to wallowing in the mire.”","understanding":"συμβέβηκεν αὐτοῖς τὸ τῆς ἀληθοῦς παροιμίας, “But it has happened to them according to the true proverb” — παροιμία, a saying. “The dog turns to his own vomit again” — from Proverbs 26:11; ἐπιστρέφω, the ‘turn back’ of James 5:19–20, here turning the wrong way. “and the sow that has washed to wallowing in the mire” — λούω, to wash; κύλισμα, rolling; βόρβορος, mud. The dog and the pig were unclean animals; washing did not change their nature. These teachers cleaned up outwardly, but their hearts were never made new.","words":[{"strongs":"G4819","hebrew":"συμβαίνω","translit":"symbaínō","english":"happened"},{"strongs":"G227","hebrew":"ἀληθής","translit":"alēthḗs","english":"true"},{"strongs":"G3942","hebrew":"παροιμία","translit":"paroimía","english":"proverb"},{"strongs":"G2965","hebrew":"κύων","translit":"kýōn","english":"dog"},{"strongs":"G1994","hebrew":"ἐπιστρέφω","translit":"epistréphō","english":"turns"},{"strongs":"G1829","hebrew":"ἐξέραμα","translit":"exérama","english":"vomit"},{"strongs":"G5300","hebrew":"ὗς","translit":"hŷs","english":"sow"},{"strongs":"G3068","hebrew":"λούω","translit":"loúō","english":"washed"},{"strongs":"G2946","hebrew":"κύλισμα","translit":"kýlisma","english":"wallowing"},{"strongs":"G1004","hebrew":"βόρβορος","translit":"bórboros","english":"mire"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"Smuggled In","verseRef":"v. 1-3","image":"Picture a city gate at dusk. Merchants pass through with carts of goods, and the guards check them casually. Hidden under bales of cloth in one cart is a sack of poison, smuggled in to be mixed into the city's food. The merchant smiles at the guards, speaks smoothly, and is waved through.","meaning":"“But false prophets also arose among the people, as false teachers will also be among you, who will secretly bring in destructive heresies, denying even the Master who bought them, bringing on themselves swift destruction” (v. 1). False teaching rarely announces itself. It comes in alongside true things, wrapped in smooth words, driven by greed (v. 3). The church must be alert at its gates."},{"title":"Rescued from the Fire","verseRef":"v. 4-9","image":"Picture a city burning under a sky raining fire. Smoke billows over the plain; buildings collapse. On a road leading away, two angels are pulling a man, his wife and his daughters by the hand, hurrying them towards the hills. Behind them the city is turning to ash.","meaning":"God did not spare the angels who sinned, the world of Noah's day or Sodom and Gomorrah — but He preserved Noah and “delivered righteous Lot” (v. 7). “the Lord knows how to deliver the godly out of temptation and to keep the unrighteous under punishment for the day of judgment” (v. 9). Whatever corruption surrounds you, God knows how to rescue you."},{"title":"Wells Without Water","verseRef":"v. 17-19","image":"Picture a traveller crossing a desert, his water skin empty. He sees a well ahead and runs to it, lowers the bucket, and pulls it up — dry. Above him, dark clouds gather, and his heart leaps at the promise of rain; but a gust of wind sweeps them away and the sky is empty again.","meaning":"“These are wells without water, clouds driven by a storm; for whom the blackness of darkness has been reserved forever” (v. 17). False teachers promise refreshment and freedom: “promising them liberty, while they themselves are bondservants of corruption” (v. 19). Test every teacher by whether they lead you to the living water of Christ or leave you thirsty and enslaved."},{"title":"The Washed Pig","verseRef":"v. 20-22","image":"Picture a farmer who scrubs his pig clean, every bristle washed. He turns his back for a moment, and the pig trots straight to the muddiest corner of the yard and rolls in it with delight. Nearby, a dog that has been sick returns to sniff and eat what it brought up.","meaning":"“But it has happened to them according to the true proverb, ‘The dog turns to his own vomit again,’ and ‘the sow that has washed to wallowing in the mire’” (v. 22). Outward cleaning does not change an inward nature. Only new birth — becoming partakers of the divine nature (1:4) — makes a person hate the mud they once loved."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"False Teachers Will Come (v. 1-3)","body":"As false prophets arose in Israel, false teachers will arise in the church, secretly bringing in destructive heresies, denying the Master who bought them; many will follow their sensuality, the way of truth will be slandered, and in greed they will exploit believers with fabricated words; their judgement is not idle."},{"label":"God Judges and Rescues (v. 4-10a)","body":"If God judged the sinning angels, the ancient world and Sodom and Gomorrah, but preserved Noah and rescued righteous Lot, then the Lord knows how to rescue the godly from trials and to keep the unrighteous for judgement, especially those who indulge the flesh and despise authority."},{"label":"Their Character Exposed (v. 10b-16)","body":"They are bold and arrogant, slandering glorious beings that even angels do not accuse; like unreasoning animals they will be destroyed; they revel in daylight, are blots at the church's feasts, have eyes full of adultery, entice unstable souls, are trained in greed, and follow the way of Balaam, who loved the wages of wrongdoing and was rebuked by a donkey."},{"label":"Empty Promises and Final Ruin (v. 17-22)","body":"They are waterless springs and wind-driven mists, uttering empty boasts and enticing new converts with promises of freedom while they themselves are slaves of corruption; those who escape the world through knowing Christ and are then entangled again are worse off than before, like a dog returning to its vomit or a washed pig to the mud."}],"sections":[{"heading":"Wolves Inside the Fold","paragraphs":["Chapter 1 ended with true prophets carried along by the Holy Spirit. Chapter 2 begins with their opposites: “But false prophets also arose among the people, as false teachers will also be among you” (v. 1). Jesus warned, “Beware of false prophets, who come to you in sheep’s clothing, but inwardly are ravening wolves” (Matthew 7:15). Paul told the Ephesian elders, “Men will arise from among your own selves, speaking perverse things, to draw away the disciples after them” (Acts 20:30). The danger is not only from outside; it grows inside the church.","Peter describes their method and motive. Their method is stealth: they “secretly bring in destructive heresies” (v. 1), mixing poison into wholesome food. Their message denies “the Master who bought them” — not necessarily in words, but in lives that reject His lordship. Their morality is sensual, and “Many will follow their immoral ways” (v. 2). And their motive is greed: “In covetousness they will exploit you with deceptive words” (v. 3). Religion becomes a business; believers become customers.","This chapter closely parallels Jude 4–18, which uses many of the same examples and images. Whether Peter used Jude, Jude used Peter, or both drew on common teaching is debated; what matters is that two apostolic voices give the same warning. The church in every age needs it, because the same pattern repeats: smooth words, a gospel without holiness, and leaders who serve their appetites rather than Christ."]},{"heading":"God Knows How to Rescue and How to Judge","paragraphs":["In the middle of the warnings, Peter builds one long sentence (vv. 4–9) on three examples from Genesis. God did not spare the angels who sinned but cast them into Tartarus to await judgement. He did not spare the ancient world but brought the flood — yet preserved Noah, “a preacher of righteousness” (v. 5). He reduced Sodom and Gomorrah to ashes — yet rescued Lot, “very distressed by the lustful life of the wicked” (v. 7).","The conclusion is the heart of the chapter: “the Lord knows how to deliver the godly out of temptation and to keep the unrighteous under punishment for the day of judgment” (v. 9). This is comfort for faithful believers surrounded by corruption, as Lot was. Lot was not perfect — Genesis shows his weakness — but he was tormented daily by the evil he saw and heard (v. 8). God saw that grief and rescued him. Jesus pointed to the same events: “Likewise, even as it was in the days of Lot: they ate, they drank, they bought, they sold, they planted, they built” (Luke 17:28), “but in the day that Lot went out from Sodom, it rained fire and sulfur from the sky, and destroyed them all” (Luke 17:29).","It is also a warning. God's patience with false teachers is not indifference: “whose sentence now from of old doesn’t linger, and their destruction will not slumber” (v. 3). The same God who judged angels, the world and cities will judge those who lead His people astray."]},{"heading":"The Anatomy of False Teaching","paragraphs":["Peter's portrait of the false teachers is vivid and harsh, in the style of the Old Testament prophets. They despise authority and slander spiritual powers that even angels treat with caution (vv. 10–11). They live like unreasoning animals, driven by instinct (v. 12). They party in broad daylight and sit at the church's love feasts as ‘spots and defects’ (v. 13) — the opposite of Christ, the spotless Lamb. Their eyes are “full of adultery”, their hearts “trained in greed” (v. 14). They follow Balaam, the prophet who loved money more than God (v. 15), and who was rebuked by his own donkey (v. 16).","Their offer is freedom: “promising them liberty, while they themselves are bondservants of corruption; for a man is brought into bondage by whoever overcomes him” (v. 19). This is the oldest lie: that freedom means having no restraint. Jesus said, “Most certainly I tell you, everyone who commits sin is the bondservant of sin” (John 8:34). Paul: “Don’t you know that when you present yourselves as servants and obey someone, you are the servants of whomever you obey” (Romans 6:16). True freedom is the freedom to obey God.","The chapter ends with a terrible verdict: those who escape the world's defilement through knowing Christ, and are then entangled again, end worse than before (vv. 20–21). The strongest reading of verse 22 is that these people never truly changed: like the washed pig and the dog, their nature remained the same, and they went back to what they were. John says the same of those who left the church: “They went out from us, but they didn’t belong to us” (1 John 2:19). But the warning is real for everyone: outward religion without inward new birth will not last."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"False Prophets, Balaam and the Last State","paragraphs":["Jesus predicted exactly what Peter describes: “For there will arise false christs, and false prophets, and they will show great signs and wonders, so as to lead astray, if possible, even the chosen ones” (Matthew 24:24). And His words about the unclean spirit returning stand behind verse 20: “The last state of that man becomes worse than the first. Even so will it be also to this evil generation” (Matthew 12:45).","Jude gives a parallel warning: “For there are certain men who crept in secretly, even those who were long ago written about for this condemnation: ungodly men, turning the grace of our God into indecency, and denying our only Master, God, and Lord, Jesus Christ” (Jude 1:4). He too names Balaam: “Woe to them! For they went in the way of Cain, and ran riotously in the error of Balaam for hire” (Jude 1:11). And the risen Christ warned the church at Pergamum about “some who hold the teaching of Balaam” (Revelation 2:14).","The ‘Master who bought them’ (v. 1) recalls Paul: “for you were bought with a price. Therefore glorify God in your body and in your spirit, which are God’s” (1 Corinthians 6:20), and heaven's song: “you were killed, and bought us for God with your blood” (Revelation 5:9). Those who have truly been bought glorify their Master; false teachers deny Him."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Heresies","hebrew":"αἵρεσις","translit":"haíresis","strongs":"G139 (secretly bring in παρεισάγω, pareiságō, G3919)","verses":"v. 1","insight":"Strong's: “properly, a choice, i.e. (specially) a party or (abstractly) disunion.” αἵρεσις first meant a choice, then a school of thought or party (the ‘sect’ of the Pharisees, Acts 15:5). In Christian use it came to mean teaching chosen in opposition to the apostolic gospel. Paul lists it among the works of the flesh (Galatians 5:20). Peter calls these heresies ‘of destruction’ — they ruin those who believe them — and says they are smuggled in, mixed with truth so as to pass unnoticed."},{"english":"Cast down to Tartarus","hebrew":"ταρταρόω","translit":"tartaróō","strongs":"G5020","verses":"v. 4","insight":"Strong's: “to incarcerate in eternal torment.” ταρταρόω is used only here in the Bible. In Greek mythology Tartarus was the deepest pit beneath Hades, where the rebel Titans were imprisoned. Peter borrows the word his readers knew to describe the prison of the angels who sinned, held until the judgement. Jude speaks of the same angels ‘kept in everlasting bonds under darkness’. The point is not mythology but certainty: if God judged rebellious angels, He will judge false teachers."},{"english":"Preserved","hebrew":"φυλάσσω","translit":"phylássō","strongs":"G5442 (reserved τηρέω, tēréō, G5083)","verses":"v. 5; 3:17","insight":"Strong's: “to watch, i.e. be on guard (literally of figuratively); by implication, to preserve, obey, avoid”. φυλάσσω and τηρέω are both in the hold family, and Peter uses both in this passage with opposite effects. τηρέω (vv. 4, 9, 17) is used of the wicked being ‘kept’ for judgement; φυλάσσω (v. 5) of Noah being ‘guarded’ for life. God watches over both, with different ends. In 3:17 Peter uses φυλάσσω again: ‘beware’ — guard yourselves."},{"english":"Trained","hebrew":"γυμνάζω","translit":"gymnázō","strongs":"G1128 (greed πλεονεξία, pleonexía, G4124)","verses":"v. 14","insight":"Strong's: “to practise naked (in the games), i.e. train (figuratively).” γυμνάζω gives us ‘gymnasium’. It belongs to the discipline family: Hebrews 12:11 uses it of believers trained by God's discipline, and 1 Timothy 4:7 of training oneself in godliness. Peter turns it upside down: the false teachers have trained their hearts — in greed. Every heart is being trained in something. The question is whether you are exercising yourself in godliness or in desire."},{"english":"Brought into bondage","hebrew":"δουλόω","translit":"doulóō","strongs":"G1402 (bondservants δοῦλος, doûlos, G1401; overcomes ἡττάω, hēttáō, G2274)","verses":"v. 19","insight":"Strong's: “to enslave (literally or figuratively).” δουλόω and δοῦλος, from the servant family, describe the false teachers' real condition: they promise freedom but are slaves of φθορά, corruption. ἡττάω means to be defeated, to lose a fight. Peter's principle is universal: whatever defeats you owns you. Believers are also δοῦλοι — of God (1 Peter 2:16) — and that is the only slavery that sets anyone free."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"In this chapter the Holy Spirit is sharpening your discernment and assuring you of His protection in a corrupt world.","items":[{"title":"Test the Teachers","body":"Not everyone who speaks about God speaks for God. The Spirit asks you to test the voices you listen to: Do they exalt Christ as Lord and Master? Do they call for holiness, or excuse sin as freedom? Are they generous servants, or do they treat people as merchandise (v. 3)? Is their life consistent with their words? Ask Him for discernment about one voice you have been trusting."},{"title":"Grieved Like Lot","body":"Lot was “tormented in his righteous soul from day to day with seeing and hearing lawless deeds” (v. 8). Does the evil around you still grieve you, or have you grown used to it? The Spirit wants to keep your conscience tender. Ask Him to restore your grief over sin — and to remind you that “the Lord knows how to deliver the godly out of temptation” (v. 9)."},{"title":"What Owns You?","body":"“for a man is brought into bondage by whoever overcomes him” (v. 19). Is there a habit, appetite or desire that regularly defeats you? Whatever defeats you owns you. The Spirit does not condemn you; He offers freedom in Christ. Name it before Him and ask for the power of the divine nature (1:4) to break its grip."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Voice Check","body":"List the teachers, preachers, writers and online voices you regularly listen to. Measure each by verses 1–3 and 18–19: Do they honour Christ's lordship, call to holiness, and serve rather than exploit?"},{"title":"The Grief Check","body":"What evil in your culture or entertainment no longer bothers you (v. 8)? What has dulled your conscience?"},{"title":"The Freedom Check","body":"Is there anything you call ‘freedom’ that is actually bondage (v. 19)? Is there an old sin you have been tempted to return to (v. 22)?"}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"A Discernment Grid","body":"Write four questions on a card: (1) Does this teaching exalt Jesus as Lord and Saviour? (2) Does it agree with the plain teaching of Scripture? (3) Does it lead to holiness and love, or excuse sin? (4) Does the teacher's life and use of money match the message? Use the card this month with every new teaching, video or book you encounter."},{"title":"Break One Chain","body":"Identify one area where you are being overcome (v. 19). This week, confess it to God and to a trusted believer, remove one access point to it, and memorise 1:3–4. Each time temptation comes, say aloud: ‘His divine power has given me everything I need for godliness. I am not a slave of corruption.’"}]},"prayerReflection":"“Lord Jesus, my Master who bought me with Your blood, I confess You as Lord. Guard me and Your church from false teachers who secretly bring in destructive heresies, who deny You with their lives and exploit Your people with smooth words. Give me discernment to recognise wells without water and clouds without rain. Thank You that You know how to rescue the godly from trials, as You preserved Noah and rescued righteous Lot. Keep my heart tender, grieved by the evil I see and hear, not hardened by it. Do not let me be enticed by promises of a freedom that is really slavery. Whatever has been overcoming me, break its power, because I belong to You. Keep me from returning to what You washed me from. Train my heart, not in greed or desire, but in godliness. And let me never deny You, my Master, in word or in life. In Your name, Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"Lot in Sodom","ref":"Genesis 19:1-29","relatedVerses":"v. 6-9","summary":"Lot had chosen to live near Sodom for its rich pasture, and in time he settled inside the city. When two angels came to warn him of judgement, the men of the city surrounded his house with violent intent. The angels struck them blind and urged Lot to flee with his family. He hesitated, and the angels seized his hand and his family's hands and brought them out, ‘Yahweh being merciful to him’. Then fire and sulphur fell, and the cities were destroyed. Lot's choices were not wise, but Peter calls him righteous and grieved by the evil around him — and God rescued him."},{"title":"Balaam and the Donkey","ref":"Numbers 22:1-35; 31:16","relatedVerses":"v. 15-16","summary":"Balak, king of Moab, offered the prophet Balaam rich rewards to curse Israel. God told Balaam not to go, but the money drew him. As he rode, the angel of the Lord stood in the road with a drawn sword. The donkey saw the angel and turned aside three times; Balaam beat her each time. Then the Lord opened the donkey's mouth, and she asked why he was beating her. Balaam's eyes were opened, and he saw the angel. Later he advised Moab to lead Israel into idolatry and immorality. Peter uses him as the pattern of the false teacher: a religious man for hire, who loved the wages of wrongdoing."},{"title":"Noah, the Preacher of Righteousness","ref":"Genesis 6:9-7:24; Hebrews 11:7","relatedVerses":"v. 5","summary":"In a world filled with violence, Noah walked with God. While he built the ark for many years, he became, as Peter calls him, ‘a preacher of righteousness’ — his words and his work warned his generation. No one but his family listened. When the flood came, God shut them in and preserved them. Hebrews says, “By faith, Noah, being warned about things not yet seen, moved with godly fear, prepared a ship for the saving of his house, through which he condemned the world” (Hebrews 11:7). Noah is proof that one faithful family can stand in a corrupt world, and that God guards those who are His."}]},{"type":"chapterStudy","book":"2 Peter","chapter":3,"chapterTitle":"The Day of the Lord and the God Who Waits","themeTitle":"“The Lord Is Not Slow Concerning His Promise … But Is Patient with Us”","scripture":[{"v":1,"text":"This is now, beloved, the second letter that I have written to you; and in both of them I stir up your sincere mind by reminding you;","understanding":"ταύτην ἤδη, ἀγαπητοί, δευτέραν ὑμῖν γράφω ἐπιστολήν, “This is now, beloved, the second letter that I have written to you” — ἀγαπητός, from the love family, used four times in this chapter; δεύτερος, second, most naturally referring to 1 Peter. ἐν αἷς διεγείρω ὑμῶν ἐν ὑπομνήσει τὴν εἰλικρινῆ διάνοιαν, “and in both of them I stir up your sincere mind by reminding you” — διεγείρω, to wake fully, as in 1:13; εἰλικρινής, pure, tested in the sunlight; διάνοια, the thinking mind of 1 Peter 1:13.","words":[{"strongs":"G27","hebrew":"ἀγαπητός","translit":"agapētós","english":"beloved"},{"strongs":"G1208","hebrew":"δεύτερος","translit":"deúteros","english":"second"},{"strongs":"G1992","hebrew":"ἐπιστολή","translit":"epistolḗ","english":"letter"},{"strongs":"G1125","hebrew":"γράφω","translit":"gráphō","english":"written"},{"strongs":"G1326","hebrew":"διεγείρω","translit":"diegeírō","english":"stir up"},{"strongs":"G1506","hebrew":"εἰλικρινής","translit":"eilikrinḗs","english":"sincere"},{"strongs":"G1271","hebrew":"διάνοια","translit":"diánoia","english":"mind"},{"strongs":"G5280","hebrew":"ὑπόμνησις","translit":"hypómnēsis","english":"reminding"}]},{"v":2,"text":"that you should remember the words which were spoken before by the holy prophets, and the commandment of us, the apostles of the Lord and Savior:","understanding":"μνησθῆναι τῶν προειρημένων ῥημάτων ὑπὸ τῶν ἁγίων προφητῶν, “that you should remember the words which were spoken before by the holy prophets” — ῥῆμα, from the word family: particular spoken words, as distinct from λόγος, the whole message (v. 5). καὶ τῆς τῶν ἀποστόλων ὑμῶν ἐντολῆς τοῦ κυρίου καὶ σωτῆρος, “and the commandment of us, the apostles of the Lord and Savior” — Old Testament prophets and New Testament apostles stand side by side as the voice of God (Ephesians 2:20).","words":[{"strongs":"G3415","hebrew":"μνάομαι","translit":"mnáomai","english":"remember"},{"strongs":"G4487","hebrew":"ῥῆμα","translit":"rhēma","english":"words"},{"strongs":"G4280","hebrew":"προερέω","translit":"proeréō","english":"spoken before"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"holy"},{"strongs":"G4396","hebrew":"προφήτης","translit":"prophḗtēs","english":"prophets"},{"strongs":"G1785","hebrew":"ἐντολή","translit":"entolḗ","english":"commandment"},{"strongs":"G652","hebrew":"ἀπόστολος","translit":"apóstolos","english":"apostles"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G4990","hebrew":"σωτήρ","translit":"sōtḗr","english":"Savior"}]},{"v":3,"text":"knowing this first, that in the last days mockers will come, walking after their own lusts,","understanding":"τοῦτο πρῶτον γινώσκοντες, “knowing this first” — γινώσκω, from the know family, the same opening as 1:20. ὅτι ἐλεύσονται ἐπ᾽ ἐσχάτων τῶν ἡμερῶν ἐμπαῖκται, “that in the last days mockers will come” — ἡμέρα, from the time family: the last days began with Christ's first coming (Acts 2:17); ἐμπαίκτης, a scoffer, one who treats holy things as a joke. κατὰ τὰς ἰδίας ἐπιθυμίας αὐτῶν πορευόμενοι, “walking after their own lusts” — the root of their scoffing is moral: denying judgement frees them to follow desire.","words":[{"strongs":"G1097","hebrew":"γινώσκω","translit":"ginṓskō","english":"knowing"},{"strongs":"G2078","hebrew":"ἔσχατος","translit":"éschatos","english":"last"},{"strongs":"G2250","hebrew":"ἡμέρα","translit":"hēméra","english":"days"},{"strongs":"G1703","hebrew":"ἐμπαίκτης","translit":"empaíktēs","english":"mockers"},{"strongs":"G1939","hebrew":"ἐπιθυμία","translit":"epithymía","english":"lusts"},{"strongs":"G4198","hebrew":"πορεύομαι","translit":"poreúomai","english":"walking"}]},{"v":4,"text":"and saying, “Where is the promise of his coming? For, from the day that the fathers fell asleep, all things continue as they were from the beginning of the creation.”","understanding":"καὶ λέγοντες, “and saying, ‘Where is the promise of his coming?’” — ἐπαγγελία, a promise; παρουσία, Christ's royal arrival, the coming Peter saw previewed on the mountain (1:16). “For, from the day that the fathers fell asleep, all things continue as they were from the beginning of the creation.” — κοιμάω, from the sleep family: the believer's word for death; διαμένω, to remain unchanged; ἀρχή, beginning — the word is also in the power family, where it means rule or principality, but here it carries its first sense, the start; κτίσις, creation. Their argument: the universe is a closed system; nothing ever breaks in.","words":[{"strongs":"G1860","hebrew":"ἐπαγγελία","translit":"epangelía","english":"promise"},{"strongs":"G3952","hebrew":"παρουσία","translit":"parousía","english":"coming"},{"strongs":"G3962","hebrew":"πατήρ","translit":"patḗr","english":"fathers"},{"strongs":"G2837","hebrew":"κοιμάω","translit":"koimáō","english":"fell asleep"},{"strongs":"G1265","hebrew":"διαμένω","translit":"diaménō","english":"continue"},{"strongs":"G746","hebrew":"ἀρχή","translit":"archḗ","english":"beginning"},{"strongs":"G2937","hebrew":"κτίσις","translit":"ktísis","english":"creation"}]},{"v":5,"text":"For this they willfully forget, that there were heavens from of old, and an earth formed out of water and amid water, by the word of God;","understanding":"λανθάνει γὰρ αὐτοὺς τοῦτο θέλοντας, “For this they willfully forget” — λανθάνω, to escape notice; θέλω, by choice. Their ignorance is deliberate. ὅτι οὐρανοὶ ἦσαν ἔκπαλαι καὶ γῆ ἐξ ὕδατος καὶ δι᾽ ὕδατος συνεστῶσα, “that there were heavens from of old, and an earth formed out of water and amid water” — γῆ, from the world family: the earth, the land; συνίστημι, to hold together (Genesis 1:6–9). τῷ τοῦ θεοῦ λόγῳ, “by the word of God” — λόγος, from the word family: God's creative command. The world has not continued unchanged; God made it by His word, and God has intervened before.","words":[{"strongs":"G2990","hebrew":"λανθάνω","translit":"lanthánō","english":"forget"},{"strongs":"G2309","hebrew":"θέλω","translit":"thélō","english":"willfully"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heavens"},{"strongs":"G1597","hebrew":"ἔκπαλαι","translit":"ékpalai","english":"from of old"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G5204","hebrew":"ὕδωρ","translit":"hýdōr","english":"water"},{"strongs":"G4921","hebrew":"συνιστάω","translit":"synistáō","english":"formed"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"word"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"}]},{"v":6,"text":"by which means the world that then was, being overflowed with water, perished.","understanding":"δι᾽ ὧν ὁ τότε κόσμος ὕδατι κατακλυσθεὶς ἀπώλετο, “by which means the world that then was, being overflowed with water, perished” — κόσμος, from the world family: the ordered world of human life, as distinct from γῆ in verse 5, the physical earth. κατακλύζω, to flood, the verb of the κατακλυσμός of 2:5; ἀπόλλυμι, to perish. Water and word made the world; water and word judged it.","words":[{"strongs":"G2889","hebrew":"κόσμος","translit":"kósmos","english":"world"},{"strongs":"G5204","hebrew":"ὕδωρ","translit":"hýdōr","english":"water"},{"strongs":"G2626","hebrew":"κατακλύζω","translit":"kataklýzō","english":"overflowed"},{"strongs":"G622","hebrew":"ἀπόλλυμι","translit":"apóllymi","english":"perished"}]},{"v":7,"text":"But the heavens that now exist, and the earth, by the same word have been stored up for fire, being reserved against the day of judgment and destruction of ungodly men.","understanding":"οἱ δὲ νῦν οὐρανοὶ καὶ ἡ γῆ τῷ αὐτῷ λόγῳ τεθησαυρισμένοι εἰσὶν πυρί, “But the heavens that now exist, and the earth, by the same word have been stored up for fire” — λόγος, from the word family: the same word that created and flooded; θησαυρίζω, to store up as treasure, the verb of James 5:3. γῆ, from the world family. τηρούμενοι εἰς ἡμέραν κρίσεως καὶ ἀπωλείας τῶν ἀσεβῶν ἀνθρώπων, “being reserved against the day of judgment and destruction of ungodly men” — τηρέω, from the hold family: kept, like the fallen angels (2:4); ἡμέρα, from the time family; ἀσεβής, from the godliness family. Flood then; fire next.","words":[{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heavens"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G3056","hebrew":"λόγος","translit":"lógos","english":"word"},{"strongs":"G2343","hebrew":"θησαυρίζω","translit":"thēsaurízō","english":"stored up"},{"strongs":"G4442","hebrew":"πῦρ","translit":"pŷr","english":"fire"},{"strongs":"G5083","hebrew":"τηρέω","translit":"tēréō","english":"reserved"},{"strongs":"G2250","hebrew":"ἡμέρα","translit":"hēméra","english":"day"},{"strongs":"G2920","hebrew":"κρίσις","translit":"krísis","english":"judgment"},{"strongs":"G684","hebrew":"ἀπώλεια","translit":"apṓleia","english":"destruction"},{"strongs":"G765","hebrew":"ἀσεβής","translit":"asebḗs","english":"ungodly"},{"strongs":"G444","hebrew":"ἄνθρωπος","translit":"ánthrōpos","english":"men"}]},{"v":8,"text":"But don’t forget this one thing, beloved, that one day is with the Lord as a thousand years, and a thousand years as one day.","understanding":"ἓν δὲ τοῦτο μὴ λανθανέτω ὑμᾶς, ἀγαπητοί, “But don’t forget this one thing, beloved” — ἀγαπητός, from the love family; λανθάνω, the same verb as verse 5: the scoffers forget deliberately; believers must not forget at all. ὅτι μία ἡμέρα παρὰ κυρίῳ ὡς χίλια ἔτη καὶ χίλια ἔτη ὡς ἡμέρα μία, “that one day is with the Lord as a thousand years, and a thousand years as one day” — echoing Psalm 90:4. ἡμέρα and ἔτος, both from the time family: God is not bound by time as we are. A thousand years do not tire Him, and a single day is enough for Him to do what centuries could not.","words":[{"strongs":"G2990","hebrew":"λανθάνω","translit":"lanthánō","english":"forget"},{"strongs":"G27","hebrew":"ἀγαπητός","translit":"agapētós","english":"beloved"},{"strongs":"G2250","hebrew":"ἡμέρα","translit":"hēméra","english":"day"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G5507","hebrew":"χίλιοι","translit":"chílioi","english":"thousand"},{"strongs":"G2094","hebrew":"ἔτος","translit":"étos","english":"years"}]},{"v":9,"text":"The Lord is not slow concerning his promise, as some count slowness; but is patient with us, not wishing that any should perish, but that all should come to repentance.","understanding":"οὐ βραδύνει κύριος τῆς ἐπαγγελίας, “The Lord is not slow concerning his promise” — βραδύνω, to delay, the verb of James's ‘slow to speak’ (James 1:19). ὥς τινες βραδύτητα ἡγοῦνται, “as some count slowness” — ἡγέομαι, to reckon. ἀλλὰ μακροθυμεῖ εἰς ὑμᾶς, “but is patient with us” — μακροθυμέω, from the endurance family: long-tempered, the patience God showed in Noah's day (1 Peter 3:20). μὴ βουλόμενός τινας ἀπολέσθαι, “not wishing that any should perish” — βούλομαι, to desire. ἀλλὰ πάντας εἰς μετάνοιαν χωρῆσαι, “but that all should come to repentance” — μετάνοια, a change of mind and direction. God's delay is mercy, giving time to turn (1 Timothy 2:4). How this desire relates to God's choosing is debated; Peter's point is that God takes no pleasure in destruction.","words":[{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G1019","hebrew":"βραδύνω","translit":"bradýnō","english":"slow"},{"strongs":"G1860","hebrew":"ἐπαγγελία","translit":"epangelía","english":"promise"},{"strongs":"G1022","hebrew":"βραδύτης","translit":"bradýtēs","english":"slowness"},{"strongs":"G2233","hebrew":"ἡγέομαι","translit":"hēgéomai","english":"count"},{"strongs":"G3114","hebrew":"μακροθυμέω","translit":"makrothyméō","english":"patient"},{"strongs":"G1014","hebrew":"βούλομαι","translit":"boúlomai","english":"wishing"},{"strongs":"G622","hebrew":"ἀπόλλυμι","translit":"apóllymi","english":"perish"},{"strongs":"G3341","hebrew":"μετάνοια","translit":"metánoia","english":"repentance"},{"strongs":"G5562","hebrew":"χωρέω","translit":"chōréō","english":"come"}]},{"v":10,"text":"But the day of the Lord will come as a thief in the night; in which the heavens will pass away with a great noise, and the elements will be dissolved with fervent heat, and the earth and the works that are in it will be burned up.","understanding":"ἥξει δὲ ἡμέρα κυρίου ὡς κλέπτης, “But the day of the Lord will come as a thief in the night” — ἡμέρα, from the time family: the day of the Lord, the Old Testament's great day of judgement; κλέπτης, a thief: unexpected, as Jesus said (Matthew 24:43). ἐν ᾗ οἱ οὐρανοὶ ῥοιζηδὸν παρελεύσονται, “in which the heavens will pass away with a great noise” — ῥοιζηδόν, with a rushing roar, the whoosh of a fire or a storm; παρέρχομαι, the ‘pass away’ of James 1:10. στοιχεῖα δὲ καυσούμενα λυθήσεται, “and the elements will be dissolved with fervent heat” — στοιχεῖον, the basic elements, or perhaps the heavenly bodies; λύω, to loosen, dissolve. “and the earth and the works that are in it will be burned up” — γῆ, from the world family; ἔργον, from the work family. The WEB follows manuscripts reading κατακαήσεται, ‘burned up’; the oldest read εὑρεθήσεται, ‘will be found’, laid bare before God. Either way, nothing will be hidden.","words":[{"strongs":"G2240","hebrew":"ἥκω","translit":"hḗkō","english":"come"},{"strongs":"G2250","hebrew":"ἡμέρα","translit":"hēméra","english":"day"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G2812","hebrew":"κλέπτης","translit":"kléptēs","english":"thief"},{"strongs":"G3571","hebrew":"νύξ","translit":"nýx","english":"night"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heavens"},{"strongs":"G3928","hebrew":"παρέρχομαι","translit":"parérchomai","english":"pass away"},{"strongs":"G4500","hebrew":"ῥοιζηδόν","translit":"rhoizēdón","english":"great noise"},{"strongs":"G4747","hebrew":"στοιχεῖον","translit":"stoicheîon","english":"elements"},{"strongs":"G2741","hebrew":"καυσόω","translit":"kausóō","english":"fervent heat"},{"strongs":"G3089","hebrew":"λύω","translit":"lýō","english":"dissolved"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G2041","hebrew":"ἔργον","translit":"érgon","english":"works"},{"strongs":"G2618","hebrew":"κατακαίω","translit":"katakaíō","english":"burned up"}]},{"v":11,"text":"Therefore since all these things will be destroyed like this, what kind of people ought you to be in holy living and godliness,","understanding":"τούτων οὖν πάντων λυομένων, “Therefore since all these things will be destroyed like this” — λύω again, dissolved. ποταποὺς δεῖ ὑπάρχειν ὑμᾶς, “what kind of people ought you to be” — ποταπός, of what sort. Peter's eschatology is always ethical: knowing the end shapes how you live now. ἐν ἁγίαις ἀναστροφαῖς καὶ εὐσεβείαις, “in holy living and godliness” — both plural in Greek, ‘holy ways of life and acts of godliness’; ἅγιος, the holiness of 1 Peter 1:15; εὐσέβεια, from the godliness family, the godliness of 1:3, 6–7.","words":[{"strongs":"G3089","hebrew":"λύω","translit":"lýō","english":"destroyed"},{"strongs":"G4217","hebrew":"ποταπός","translit":"potapós","english":"what kind"},{"strongs":"G40","hebrew":"ἅγιος","translit":"hágios","english":"holy"},{"strongs":"G391","hebrew":"ἀναστροφή","translit":"anastrophḗ","english":"living"},{"strongs":"G2150","hebrew":"εὐσέβεια","translit":"eusébeia","english":"godliness"}]},{"v":12,"text":"looking for and earnestly desiring the coming of the day of God, which will cause the burning heavens to be dissolved, and the elements will melt with fervent heat?","understanding":"προσδοκῶντας καὶ σπεύδοντας τὴν παρουσίαν τῆς τοῦ θεοῦ ἡμέρας, “looking for and earnestly desiring the coming of the day of God” — προσδοκάω, to wait expectantly; σπεύδω, to hurry, to hasten, or to long for: believers may even ‘hasten’ the day by holy living, prayer and witness, since God waits for repentance (v. 9). παρουσία, the coming; ἡμέρα, from the time family. δι᾽ ἣν οὐρανοὶ πυρούμενοι λυθήσονται καὶ στοιχεῖα καυσούμενα τήκεται, “which will cause the burning heavens to be dissolved, and the elements will melt with fervent heat?” — πυρόω, to set on fire; τήκω, to melt, as wax melts (Isaiah 34:4).","words":[{"strongs":"G4328","hebrew":"προσδοκάω","translit":"prosdokáō","english":"looking for"},{"strongs":"G4692","hebrew":"σπεύδω","translit":"speúdō","english":"earnestly desiring"},{"strongs":"G3952","hebrew":"παρουσία","translit":"parousía","english":"coming"},{"strongs":"G2250","hebrew":"ἡμέρα","translit":"hēméra","english":"day"},{"strongs":"G2316","hebrew":"θεός","translit":"theós","english":"God"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heavens"},{"strongs":"G4448","hebrew":"πυρόω","translit":"pyróō","english":"burning"},{"strongs":"G3089","hebrew":"λύω","translit":"lýō","english":"dissolved"},{"strongs":"G4747","hebrew":"στοιχεῖον","translit":"stoicheîon","english":"elements"},{"strongs":"G2741","hebrew":"καυσόω","translit":"kausóō","english":"fervent heat"},{"strongs":"G5080","hebrew":"τήκω","translit":"tḗkō","english":"melt"}]},{"v":13,"text":"But, according to his promise, we look for new heavens and a new earth, in which righteousness dwells.","understanding":"καινοὺς δὲ οὐρανοὺς καὶ γῆν καινὴν κατὰ τὸ ἐπάγγελμα αὐτοῦ προσδοκῶμεν, “But, according to his promise, we look for new heavens and a new earth” — καινός, from the new family: new in kind and quality, renewed, not merely νέος, new in time. The promise of Isaiah 65:17 and 66:22. γῆ, from the world family. ἐν οἷς δικαιοσύνη κατοικεῖ, “in which righteousness dwells” — κατοικέω, to settle down and live permanently. In this world righteousness is a stranger, like Lot in Sodom (2:8); in the new creation it is at home.","words":[{"strongs":"G2537","hebrew":"καινός","translit":"kainós","english":"new"},{"strongs":"G3772","hebrew":"οὐρανός","translit":"ouranós","english":"heavens"},{"strongs":"G1093","hebrew":"γῆ","translit":"gē","english":"earth"},{"strongs":"G1862","hebrew":"ἐπάγγελμα","translit":"epángelma","english":"promise"},{"strongs":"G4328","hebrew":"προσδοκάω","translit":"prosdokáō","english":"look for"},{"strongs":"G1343","hebrew":"δικαιοσύνη","translit":"dikaiosýnē","english":"righteousness"},{"strongs":"G2730","hebrew":"κατοικέω","translit":"katoikéō","english":"dwells"}]},{"v":14,"text":"Therefore, beloved, seeing that you look for these things, be diligent to be found in peace, without defect and blameless in his sight.","understanding":"διό, ἀγαπητοί, ταῦτα προσδοκῶντες, “Therefore, beloved, seeing that you look for these things” — ἀγαπητός, from the love family. σπουδάσατε, “be diligent” — σπουδάζω, the word of 1:10 and 1:15. ἄσπιλοι καὶ ἀμώμητοι αὐτῷ εὑρεθῆναι ἐν εἰρήνῃ, “to be found in peace, without defect and blameless in his sight” — ἄσπιλος, without spot, the word for Christ the spotless Lamb (1 Peter 1:19) and the exact opposite of the false teachers, ‘spots and defects’ (2:13); ἀμώμητος, without blame. εἰρήνη, peace with God and with others. Hope purifies (1 John 3:3).","words":[{"strongs":"G27","hebrew":"ἀγαπητός","translit":"agapētós","english":"beloved"},{"strongs":"G4328","hebrew":"προσδοκάω","translit":"prosdokáō","english":"look for"},{"strongs":"G4704","hebrew":"σπουδάζω","translit":"spoudázō","english":"be diligent"},{"strongs":"G784","hebrew":"ἄσπιλος","translit":"áspilos","english":"without defect"},{"strongs":"G298","hebrew":"ἀμώμητος","translit":"amṓmētos","english":"blameless"},{"strongs":"G2147","hebrew":"εὑρίσκω","translit":"heurískō","english":"found"},{"strongs":"G1515","hebrew":"εἰρήνη","translit":"eirḗnē","english":"peace"}]},{"v":15,"text":"Regard the patience of our Lord as salvation; even as our beloved brother Paul also, according to the wisdom given to him, wrote to you;","understanding":"καὶ τὴν τοῦ κυρίου ἡμῶν μακροθυμίαν σωτηρίαν ἡγεῖσθε, “Regard the patience of our Lord as salvation” — μακροθυμία, from the endurance family, as in verse 9: every day of delay is another day of opportunity to be saved; σωτηρία, salvation. καθὼς καὶ ὁ ἀγαπητὸς ἡμῶν ἀδελφὸς Παῦλος, “even as our beloved brother Paul also” — ἀγαπητός, from the love family: the man who once rebuked Peter to his face (Galatians 2:11) he now calls ‘beloved brother’. κατὰ τὴν δοθεῖσαν αὐτῷ σοφίαν ἔγραψεν ὑμῖν, “according to the wisdom given to him, wrote to you” — σοφία, from the wisdom family: a gift from God (James 1:5).","words":[{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G3115","hebrew":"μακροθυμία","translit":"makrothymía","english":"patience"},{"strongs":"G4991","hebrew":"σωτηρία","translit":"sōtēría","english":"salvation"},{"strongs":"G2233","hebrew":"ἡγέομαι","translit":"hēgéomai","english":"Regard"},{"strongs":"G27","hebrew":"ἀγαπητός","translit":"agapētós","english":"beloved"},{"strongs":"G80","hebrew":"ἀδελφός","translit":"adelphós","english":"brother"},{"strongs":"G3972","hebrew":"Παῦλος","translit":"Paûlos","english":"Paul"},{"strongs":"G4678","hebrew":"σοφία","translit":"sophía","english":"wisdom"},{"strongs":"G1325","hebrew":"δίδωμι","translit":"dídōmi","english":"given"},{"strongs":"G1125","hebrew":"γράφω","translit":"gráphō","english":"wrote"}]},{"v":16,"text":"as also in all of his letters, speaking in them of these things. In those, there are some things that are hard to understand, which the ignorant and unsettled twist, as they also do to the other Scriptures, to their own destruction.","understanding":"ὡς καὶ ἐν πάσαις ταῖς ἐπιστολαῖς, “as also in all of his letters” — Peter knows a collection of Paul's letters. ἐν αἷς ἐστιν δυσνόητά τινα, “In those, there are some things that are hard to understand” — δυσνόητος, difficult to grasp: even an apostle found Paul hard at times. ἃ οἱ ἀμαθεῖς καὶ ἀστήρικτοι στρεβλοῦσιν, “which the ignorant and unsettled twist” — ἀμαθής, untaught; ἀστήρικτος, unstable, the word of 2:14; στρεβλόω, to wrench, as on a rack. ὡς καὶ τὰς λοιπὰς γραφάς, “as they also do to the other Scriptures” — γραφή: Peter places Paul's letters alongside the Scriptures, a striking witness to their authority. πρὸς τὴν ἰδίαν αὐτῶν ἀπώλειαν, “to their own destruction.”","words":[{"strongs":"G1992","hebrew":"ἐπιστολή","translit":"epistolḗ","english":"letters"},{"strongs":"G2980","hebrew":"λαλέω","translit":"laléō","english":"speaking"},{"strongs":"G1425","hebrew":"δυσνόητος","translit":"dysnóētos","english":"hard to understand"},{"strongs":"G261","hebrew":"ἀμαθής","translit":"amathḗs","english":"ignorant"},{"strongs":"G793","hebrew":"ἀστήρικτος","translit":"astḗriktos","english":"unsettled"},{"strongs":"G4761","hebrew":"στρεβλόω","translit":"streblóō","english":"twist"},{"strongs":"G3062","hebrew":"λοιποί","translit":"loipoí","english":"other"},{"strongs":"G1124","hebrew":"γραφή","translit":"graphḗ","english":"Scriptures"},{"strongs":"G684","hebrew":"ἀπώλεια","translit":"apṓleia","english":"destruction"}]},{"v":17,"text":"You therefore, beloved, knowing these things beforehand, beware, lest being carried away with the error of the wicked, you fall from your own steadfastness.","understanding":"ὑμεῖς οὖν, ἀγαπητοί, προγινώσκοντες, “You therefore, beloved, knowing these things beforehand” — ἀγαπητός, from the love family, a fourth time; προγινώσκω, to know in advance: forewarned is forearmed. φυλάσσεσθε, “beware” — φυλάσσω, from the hold family: guard yourselves, the word used of God guarding Noah (2:5). ἵνα μὴ τῇ τῶν ἀθέσμων πλάνῃ συναπαχθέντες, “lest being carried away with the error of the wicked” — ἄθεσμος, lawless, the word of 2:7; πλάνη, error; συναπάγω, to be swept away together, as Barnabas was ‘carried away’ (Galatians 2:13). ἐκπέσητε τοῦ ἰδίου στηριγμοῦ, “you fall from your own steadfastness” — ἐκπίπτω, to fall off, like the flower of 1 Peter 1:24; στηριγμός, firm footing, the noun of στηρίζω (1:12) and the opposite of ἀστήρικτος (v. 16).","words":[{"strongs":"G27","hebrew":"ἀγαπητός","translit":"agapētós","english":"beloved"},{"strongs":"G4267","hebrew":"προγινώσκω","translit":"proginṓskō","english":"knowing these things beforehand"},{"strongs":"G5442","hebrew":"φυλάσσω","translit":"phylássō","english":"beware"},{"strongs":"G4106","hebrew":"πλάνη","translit":"plánē","english":"error"},{"strongs":"G113","hebrew":"ἄθεσμος","translit":"áthesmos","english":"wicked"},{"strongs":"G4879","hebrew":"συναπάγω","translit":"synapágō","english":"carried away"},{"strongs":"G1601","hebrew":"ἐκπίπτω","translit":"ekpíptō","english":"fall"},{"strongs":"G4740","hebrew":"στηριγμός","translit":"stērigmós","english":"steadfastness"}]},{"v":18,"text":"But grow in the grace and knowledge of our Lord and Savior Jesus Christ. To him be the glory both now and forever. Amen.","understanding":"αὐξάνετε δὲ ἐν χάριτι καὶ γνώσει τοῦ κυρίου ἡμῶν καὶ σωτῆρος Ἰησοῦ Χριστοῦ, “But grow in the grace and knowledge of our Lord and Savior Jesus Christ” — αὐξάνω, to grow, the word of 1 Peter 2:2: newborns growing by the word. γνῶσις, from the know family, as in 1:5–6. The defence against falling is not standing still but growing. αὐτῷ ἡ δόξα καὶ νῦν καὶ εἰς ἡμέραν αἰῶνος, “To him be the glory both now and forever” — literally ‘to the day of the age’: ἡμέρα, from the time family, and αἰών, in the time family and the world family, the endless day that dawns when the morning star rises (1:19). Glory is given to Jesus as to God. Ἀμήν. These are among the last recorded words of Peter.","words":[{"strongs":"G837","hebrew":"αὐξάνω","translit":"auxánō","english":"grow"},{"strongs":"G5485","hebrew":"χάρις","translit":"cháris","english":"grace"},{"strongs":"G1108","hebrew":"γνῶσις","translit":"gnōsis","english":"knowledge"},{"strongs":"G2962","hebrew":"κύριος","translit":"kýrios","english":"Lord"},{"strongs":"G4990","hebrew":"σωτήρ","translit":"sōtḗr","english":"Savior"},{"strongs":"G1391","hebrew":"δόξα","translit":"dóxa","english":"glory"},{"strongs":"G2250","hebrew":"ἡμέρα","translit":"hēméra","english":"forever"},{"strongs":"G165","hebrew":"αἰών","translit":"aiṓn","english":"forever"}]}],"visualMeditations":[{"title":"The Scoffers' Closed Box","verseRef":"v. 3-7","image":"Picture a group of men sitting comfortably in a sealed room, sure that nothing outside can affect them. ‘Nothing has changed since our grandfathers,’ they laugh. ‘The sun rises, the seasons turn, people are born and die. All this talk of a coming King is a fairy tale.’ Outside the room, unseen, the water once rose to the rooftops; and now a fire is being gathered.","meaning":"“Where is the promise of his coming? For, from the day that the fathers fell asleep, all things continue as they were from the beginning of the creation” (v. 4). But they forget on purpose that God created the world by His word and once judged it by water. “But the heavens that now exist, and the earth, by the same word have been stored up for fire, being reserved against the day of judgment and destruction of ungodly men” (v. 7). The world is not a closed box. God has broken in before, and He will again."},{"title":"The Patient Father at the Door","verseRef":"v. 8-9","image":"Picture a father standing at the door of his house every evening, looking down the road. His son left years ago. Neighbours say he should give up and lock the door. But each night he waits a little longer, leaving the lamp burning, because he does not want his son to come home to a closed house.","meaning":"“The Lord is not slow concerning his promise, as some count slowness; but is patient with us, not wishing that any should perish, but that all should come to repentance” (v. 9). What looks like delay is mercy. Every day the Lord does not return is another day for someone to come home. “Regard the patience of our Lord as salvation” (v. 15)."},{"title":"The Thief in the Night","verseRef":"v. 10-12","image":"Picture a house asleep at three in the morning. No alarm, no warning — just a sudden crash, and everything changes. Then picture the whole sky lit with fire, the roar of the heavens passing away, the elements melting like wax, and every work of humankind laid bare.","meaning":"“But the day of the Lord will come as a thief in the night; in which the heavens will pass away with a great noise, and the elements will be dissolved with fervent heat, and the earth and the works that are in it will be burned up” (v. 10). Peter's question follows immediately: “Therefore since all these things will be destroyed like this, what kind of people ought you to be in holy living and godliness” (v. 11). Knowing the end changes how you live today."},{"title":"Where Righteousness Is at Home","verseRef":"v. 13-14","image":"Picture a refugee who has lived for years as a stranger — never quite fitting in, always slightly afraid, grieved by the injustice around him. One day he is given the keys to a house in a new country where justice is the law of the land, where no one is oppressed, and where he finally belongs. He walks through the door and weeps.","meaning":"“But, according to his promise, we look for new heavens and a new earth, in which righteousness dwells” (v. 13). Here, righteousness is a foreigner, like Lot in Sodom. There, it will be at home. “Therefore, beloved, seeing that you look for these things, be diligent to be found in peace, without defect and blameless in his sight” (v. 14). Live now as citizens of the world that is coming."}],"commentary":{"structure":[{"label":"Remember the Word (v. 1-2)","body":"In this second letter Peter stirs up their sincere minds to remember the words of the holy prophets and the command of the Lord given through the apostles."},{"label":"The Scoffers Answered (v. 3-7)","body":"In the last days scoffers will come, following their own desires and mocking the promise of Christ's coming, claiming everything continues unchanged; but they deliberately forget that God made the world by His word and destroyed it with water, and by the same word the present heavens and earth are reserved for fire on the day of judgement."},{"label":"God's Timing and Patience (v. 8-10)","body":"With the Lord one day is as a thousand years and a thousand years as one day; He is not slow but patient, not wanting anyone to perish but all to come to repentance; yet the day of the Lord will come like a thief, when the heavens pass away, the elements dissolve and the earth and its works are exposed."},{"label":"Living in the Light of the End (v. 11-16)","body":"Since everything will be dissolved, live in holiness and godliness, waiting for and hastening the day of God, looking for new heavens and a new earth where righteousness lives; be diligent to be found spotless and at peace, counting the Lord's patience as salvation, as Paul also wrote, though some of his writings are hard and are twisted by the unstable, as they twist the other Scriptures."},{"label":"Final Warning and Doxology (v. 17-18)","body":"Being forewarned, guard yourselves so as not to be carried away by the error of the lawless and lose your stability; grow in the grace and knowledge of our Lord and Saviour Jesus Christ, to whom be glory now and forever."}],"sections":[{"heading":"Where Is the Promise?","paragraphs":["Peter's last chapter answers the scoffers. He reminds his readers of “the words which were spoken before by the holy prophets, and the commandment of us, the apostles of the Lord and Savior” (v. 2), and warns that “in the last days mockers will come, walking after their own lusts” (v. 3). Their question sounds reasonable: “Where is the promise of his coming? For, from the day that the fathers fell asleep, all things continue as they were from the beginning of the creation” (v. 4). Jesus had warned of the servant who says, “My lord is delaying his coming” (Matthew 24:48).","Peter exposes two things. First, their motive: they walk after their own desires. Denying a future judgement is convenient for those who want to live without accountability. Second, their selective memory: “For this they willfully forget” (v. 5). The world has not continued unchanged. It was made by God's word, and it was flooded by God's judgement in Noah's day (v. 6). The God who created and judged the world by His word has reserved the present heavens and earth “for fire, being reserved against the day of judgment and destruction of ungodly men” (v. 7).","Peter's argument is a lesson in how to answer doubt. He does not ignore the question. He goes back to what God has already done in history — creation and the flood — and shows that the same word that did those things has promised the next. History is not a closed circle; it is a story with a Creator, a judgement and a coming King."]},{"heading":"God's Time and God's Patience","paragraphs":["Then Peter turns to believers who may be troubled by the delay: “But don’t forget this one thing, beloved, that one day is with the Lord as a thousand years, and a thousand years as one day” (v. 8). God does not experience time as we do. What seems long to us is not long to Him, and He can accomplish in a day what we could not in centuries.","More importantly, the delay has a purpose: “The Lord is not slow concerning his promise, as some count slowness; but is patient with us, not wishing that any should perish, but that all should come to repentance” (v. 9). God's delay is His patience. Paul says the same: “Or do you despise the riches of his goodness, forbearance, and patience, not knowing that the goodness of God leads you to repentance?” (Romans 2:4), and God “desires all people to be saved and come to full knowledge of the truth” (1 Timothy 2:4). Christians differ on how God's desire for all to repent relates to His choosing of His people; Peter's point is plain: God takes no pleasure in anyone's destruction, and every day of delay is a gift.","But patience is not cancellation. “But the day of the Lord will come as a thief in the night” (v. 10). Jesus used the same image: “But know this, that if the master of the house had known in what watch of the night the thief was coming, he would have watched” (Matthew 24:43). Paul too: “For you yourselves know well that the day of the Lord comes like a thief in the night” (1 Thessalonians 5:2). The heavens will pass away, the elements dissolve, and the earth and all human works will be exposed — burned up, or ‘found out’, as the oldest manuscripts read. Nothing will be hidden."]},{"heading":"What Kind of People Ought You to Be?","paragraphs":["Peter never teaches about the end without applying it to the present. “Therefore since all these things will be destroyed like this, what kind of people ought you to be in holy living and godliness” (v. 11). If everything you can see will be dissolved, it is foolish to live for what you can see. The question is not ‘When will the end come?’ but ‘What kind of person will I be when it does?’","Believers are “looking for and earnestly desiring the coming of the day of God” (v. 12). The word σπεύδω can mean ‘eagerly desiring’ or ‘hastening’. Since God waits for repentance, the church's holiness, prayer and witness are in some sense bound up with the timing of the end. And they look not for destruction alone but for renewal: “But, according to his promise, we look for new heavens and a new earth, in which righteousness dwells” (v. 13). John saw it: “I saw a new heaven and a new earth: for the first heaven and the first earth have passed away, and the sea is no more” (Revelation 21:1). Paul says creation itself “will be delivered from the bondage of decay into the liberty of the glory of the children of God” (Romans 8:21).","So Peter calls for diligent holiness: “Therefore, beloved, seeing that you look for these things, be diligent to be found in peace, without defect and blameless in his sight” (v. 14). The false teachers were ‘spots and defects’ (2:13); believers are to be spotless like their Lord. John adds, “Everyone who has this hope set on him purifies himself, even as he is pure” (1 John 3:3). Hope does not make Christians passive; it makes them holy."]},{"heading":"Paul, the Scriptures, and the Last Word","paragraphs":["Peter adds a remarkable testimony to Paul: “even as our beloved brother Paul also, according to the wisdom given to him, wrote to you” (v. 15). The two apostles had once clashed at Antioch, when Paul “resisted him to his face” (Galatians 2:11). Now Peter calls him ‘beloved brother’ and honours his God-given wisdom. He even admits that Paul's letters contain “some things that are hard to understand, which the ignorant and unsettled twist, as they also do to the other Scriptures, to their own destruction” (v. 16). By saying ‘the other Scriptures’, Peter places Paul's letters alongside the Old Testament as the word of God.","His final warning gathers the whole letter: “You therefore, beloved, knowing these things beforehand, beware, lest being carried away with the error of the wicked, you fall from your own steadfastness” (v. 17). The unstable twist Scripture; the stable stand firm. The word for ‘steadfastness’ comes from the same root as ‘established’ (1:12) — the word Jesus used when He told Peter to strengthen his brothers.","And his very last words are a command and a doxology: “But grow in the grace and knowledge of our Lord and Savior Jesus Christ. To him be the glory both now and forever. Amen” (v. 18). The letter began with grace and peace multiplied in the knowledge of God (1:2); it ends with grace and knowledge growing. The defence against error is not merely knowing what is false, but growing in Christ. And the final glory goes to Jesus — the same glory Peter saw on the mountain."]}],"ntConnection":{"heading":"The Thief, the New Creation and the Unshakeable Kingdom","paragraphs":["Jesus promised that His words would outlast the universe: “Heaven and earth will pass away, but my words will not pass away” (Matthew 24:35). Peter shows the same: the present heavens and earth are kept by God's word for judgement, but His promise of a new creation stands. Hebrews says, “Yet once more I will shake not only the earth, but also the heavens” (Hebrews 12:26), and explains that this signifies “the removing of those things that are shaken, as of things that have been made, that those things which are not shaken may remain” (Hebrews 12:27).","The new heavens and new earth of verse 13 are the climax of Revelation: “I saw a new heaven and a new earth: for the first heaven and the first earth have passed away, and the sea is no more” (Revelation 21:1), and “He will wipe away every tear from their eyes. Death will be no more; neither will there be mourning, nor crying, nor pain, any more. The first things have passed away” (Revelation 21:4). The risen Christ also warned, “If therefore you won’t watch, I will come as a thief, and you won’t know what hour I will come upon you” (Revelation 3:3).","Peter's call to holy living in view of the end echoes Paul: we are to live “soberly, righteously, and godly in this present age” (Titus 2:12), “looking for the blessed hope and appearing of the glory of our great God and Savior, Jesus Christ” (Titus 2:13). “For our citizenship is in heaven, from where we also wait for a Savior, the Lord Jesus Christ” (Philippians 3:20). Peter's last letter ends where the whole New Testament points: grow in Christ while you wait for Him."]}},"lexicon":[{"english":"Mockers / scoffers","hebrew":"ἐμπαίκτης","translit":"empaíktēs","strongs":"G1703","verses":"v. 3","insight":"Strong's: “a derider, i.e. (by implication) a false teacher.” ἐμπαίκτης comes from ἐμπαίζω, to mock, the word used of the soldiers mocking Jesus. Jude uses it of the same people (Jude 18). The dictionary's note is revealing: scoffing and false teaching go together. The scoffers in Peter's day mocked the promise of Christ's return; their descendants still do. Peter says their scoffing grows from their desires: if there is no coming Judge, they can live as they like."},{"english":"Patient","hebrew":"μακροθυμέω","translit":"makrothyméō","strongs":"G3114 (patience μακροθυμία, makrothymía, G3115)","verses":"v. 9, 15","insight":"Strong's: “to be long-spirited, i.e. (objectively) forbearing or (subjectively) patient.” μακροθυμέω and μακροθυμία, from the endurance family, describe God's long temper — His refusal to act in quick anger. James used it of the farmer waiting for rain (James 5:7); Peter uses it of God waiting for sinners. Paul writes that God “endured with much patience vessels of wrath” (Romans 9:22). God's patience is not weakness or forgetfulness. It is mercy holding back judgement so that more can be saved."},{"english":"New","hebrew":"καινός","translit":"kainós","strongs":"G2537","verses":"v. 13","insight":"Strong's derivation: ‘new (especially in freshness)’, while νέος is new ‘with respect to age’. καινός, from the new family, means new in kind and quality — renewed, fresh, different — as distinct from νέος, new in time. The new heavens and new earth are not simply a second universe made from nothing, but creation renewed and set free (Romans 8:21). It is the same word as the ‘new covenant’, the ‘new creation’ (2 Corinthians 5:17) and ‘Behold, I am making all things new’ (Revelation 21:5)."},{"english":"Earnestly desiring / hastening","hebrew":"σπεύδω","translit":"speúdō","strongs":"G4692","verses":"v. 12","insight":"Strong's: “to \"speed\" (\"study\"), i.e. urge on (diligently or earnestly); by implication, to await eagerly”. σπεύδω means to hurry. It can be used of eager longing, as the WEB has it, or of actively hastening something. Since God delays the end to allow repentance (v. 9), the church's holiness, prayer (‘Your kingdom come’) and witness may in some sense hasten the day. Either way, believers do not merely wait passively; they wait with eagerness that shapes their lives."},{"english":"Steadfastness","hebrew":"στηριγμός","translit":"stērigmós","strongs":"G4740 (unsettled ἀστήρικτος, astḗriktos, G793)","verses":"v. 17","insight":"Strong's: “stability (figuratively).” στηριγμός is the noun of στηρίζω, to establish, the word Jesus used when He told Peter to strengthen his brothers (Luke 22:32), and which Peter used of believers ‘established in the present truth’ (1:12). Its opposite, ἀστήρικτος, ‘unstable’, describes the souls false teachers entice (2:14) and those who twist Scripture (3:16). Peter's last letter is his final act of establishing the brothers — and the way to keep your footing is to grow."}],"holySpiritRevelation":{"intro":"In this final chapter the Holy Spirit lifts your eyes to the end of history — and asks what kind of person you are becoming while you wait.","items":[{"title":"Have You Stopped Expecting Him?","body":"The scoffers say nothing changes. Many believers do not mock, but they quietly stop expecting Christ to return. The Spirit asks: do you live as though this world is permanent? “But the day of the Lord will come as a thief in the night” (v. 10). What would change in your priorities, spending and relationships if you truly expected Him?"},{"title":"Who Is God Waiting For?","body":"God's patience is “not wishing that any should perish, but that all should come to repentance” (v. 9). The Spirit brings someone to mind who has not yet come to Christ. Every day of delay is a day of mercy for them. Will you pray for them by name, and look for an opportunity to speak to them of the hope you have?"},{"title":"Are You Still Growing?","body":"Peter's last words are “grow in the grace and knowledge of our Lord and Savior Jesus Christ” (v. 18). The Spirit wants to keep you steady — not by making you rigid, but by making you grow. Where are you standing still? Ask Him to show you the next step of growth in grace and in knowing Christ."}]},"lifeApplication":{"auditIntro":"Ask yourself deeply and honestly:","audit":[{"title":"The Expectation Check","body":"How often do you think about Christ's return? Does it shape your choices, or is it only a doctrine you agree with (vv. 10–12)?"},{"title":"The Holiness Check","body":"If the Lord came today, would He find you ‘in peace, without defect and blameless’ (v. 14)? What needs to be put right before then?"},{"title":"The Stability Check","body":"Are you easily carried away by new teachings, trends or arguments (v. 17)? How firmly are you rooted in Scripture — including the hard parts (v. 16)?"}],"practicesIntro":"Live this chapter by:","practices":[{"title":"Live in the Light of the Day","body":"Each morning this week, before you plan your day, read verses 10–14 and ask: ‘Lord, what kind of person ought I to be today?’ Choose one concrete act of holiness or godliness for that day — a temptation to refuse, a wrong to put right, a kindness to show. In the evening, thank God that His patience is still giving you time."},{"title":"Pray for the Ones He Is Waiting For","body":"Write down the names of three people who do not yet know Christ. Each day this month, pray verse 9 over them: ‘Lord, You are not willing that they should perish. Bring them to repentance.’ Ask God for one opportunity to share the hope of the new heavens and new earth with one of them."}]},"prayerReflection":"“Lord Jesus, I remember the words of the holy prophets and the commands You gave through Your apostles. In a world that mocks Your coming, keep me from forgetting what You have done — You made the world by Your word, You judged it in the flood, and by the same word You have reserved this world for the day of judgement. Thank You that You are not slow, but patient, not wanting anyone to perish but all to come to repentance. Let me count Your patience as salvation, and use every day of it well. I know that Your day will come like a thief in the night. So make me the kind of person I ought to be — holy in my living and godly in my ways. Make me eager for Your coming, and let my life hasten it. I look for new heavens and a new earth, where righteousness is at home. Make me diligent to be found by You in peace, spotless and blameless. Guard me from being carried away by the error of the lawless, and keep my footing firm. Thank You for the Scriptures, including the letters of Your servant Paul. Help me never to twist them, but to grow in Your grace and in knowing You, my Lord and Saviour. To You be the glory, now and to the day of eternity. Amen.”","storyConnections":[{"title":"The Flood: Water, Word and Judgement","ref":"Genesis 1:6-10; 7:11-24","relatedVerses":"v. 5-7","summary":"In the beginning God spoke, and the waters were separated, and dry land appeared — a world formed out of water and by water through His word. Centuries later, when wickedness filled the earth, the same God opened the fountains of the deep and the windows of heaven, and the waters covered even the mountains. Everything outside the ark perished. The scoffers in Peter's day claimed nothing ever changes. Peter points them to the flood: the God who spoke the world into being has already judged it once, and His word has reserved the present world for fire."},{"title":"The Faithful and Wicked Servants","ref":"Matthew 24:42-51","relatedVerses":"v. 3-4, 10-14","summary":"Jesus told of a master who left his servants in charge of his household. The faithful servant kept working and caring for the household, ready for his master's return at any time. The wicked servant said in his heart, “My lord is delaying his coming” (Matthew 24:48), and began to beat his fellow servants and eat and drink with drunkards. The master came on a day he did not expect and at an hour he did not know. Peter's scoffers are that wicked servant: they mistake delay for absence. Peter calls believers to be the faithful servant, living in holiness and peace whenever He comes."},{"title":"Peter and Paul at Antioch","ref":"Galatians 2:11-14; Acts 15:6-11","relatedVerses":"v. 15-16","summary":"At Antioch, Paul confronted Peter publicly for withdrawing from Gentile believers when men came from Jerusalem: “But when Peter came to Antioch, I resisted him to his face, because he stood condemned” (Galatians 2:11). It was a painful moment between two great apostles. Yet at the Jerusalem council Peter stood up to defend salvation by grace for the Gentiles, and years later, in his last letter, he calls Paul ‘our beloved brother’ and honours the wisdom God gave him. The rebuke had not broken their fellowship; it had been received. Humility, truth and love outlasted the conflict."}]}],"review":{"type":"finalReview","book":"2 Peter","titleEmojis":"🕯️🌅","bigPicture":{"subtitle":"Grow in Grace and Knowledge Until the Day Dawns","thesis":"If we had to summarize the Book of 2 Peter in one overarching thought, it is this: God has given you everything you need for life and godliness, so grow diligently in the knowledge of Christ, anchor yourself in the eyewitness testimony of the apostles and the Spirit-breathed Scriptures, reject teachers who promise freedom while living in corruption, and live holy lives as you wait for the day of the Lord and the new heavens and new earth where righteousness dwells.","paragraphs":["Second Peter is the last testament of an apostle who knew his death was near. He had been told by Jesus how he would die, and now the time had come to fold his tent (1:14). He did not use his last letter to talk about himself. He used it to remind, warn and strengthen the churches — to do what Jesus had told him to do: “You, when once you have turned again, establish your brothers” (Luke 22:32).","The letter faces a different threat from 1 Peter. There the danger was persecution from outside; here it is deception from inside. False teachers had arisen, clever and greedy, promising liberty while living as slaves of corruption, and mocking the promise of Christ's return. Peter answers them with three things: God's full provision for godliness (chapter 1), God's certain judgement and rescue (chapter 2), and God's patient but certain coming day (chapter 3).","Running through every chapter is one word: knowledge — not the proud ‘knowledge’ of the false teachers, but the personal, life-changing knowledge of Jesus Christ. The letter opens with grace and peace “multiplied in the knowledge of God and of Jesus our Lord” (2 Peter 1:2), and closes with “But grow in the grace and knowledge of our Lord and Savior Jesus Christ” (2 Peter 3:18). The answer to false teaching is not suspicion alone but growth.","The man who once walked on water and sank, who confessed Christ and denied Him, who slept in Gethsemane and drew a sword, ends his life as a steady shepherd. He has seen the majesty on the mountain, the cross on the hill and the empty tomb, and he faces his own death calmly, calling it an exodus. His final word is not about himself at all: “To him be the glory both now and forever. Amen” (2 Peter 3:18)."]},"map":{"intro":"2 Peter is the twenty-second book of the New Testament, the third of the letters written to the whole church. It stands between 1 Peter and the letters of John, and it shares much of its second chapter with Jude.","sections":[{"title":"From Suffering to Deception","body":"1 Peter prepared believers for the fiery trial of persecution; 2 Peter prepares them for the quieter danger of false teaching. Both letters close with the same call: be watchful and stand firm. In 1 Peter the devil prowls like a roaring lion (1 Peter 5:8); in 2 Peter he works through teachers with smooth words and greedy hearts. Together the two letters equip the church to stand against both the sword and the lie."},{"title":"The Apostolic Testimony and the Canon","body":"2 Peter makes some of the clearest statements in the New Testament about how God speaks. The apostles were eyewitnesses, not myth-makers (1:16). The prophets spoke as they were carried along by the Holy Spirit (1:21). And Paul's letters, though sometimes hard, are placed alongside ‘the other Scriptures’ (3:16). Questions about the letter's authorship have been raised since early times, and many scholars still doubt it; this study has read it as it presents itself — the final word of the apostle Peter — and as the church received it."},{"title":"Hard Passages Read Honestly","body":"‘Partakers of the divine nature’ (1:4) means sharing God's life and character, not becoming God. ‘The Master who bought them’ (2:1) is read in different ways — whether Christ's death was for those who finally perish, or whether these teachers only professed to be redeemed. Tartarus (2:4) is a Greek word Peter borrows for the prison of fallen angels. The dog and the sow (2:22) suggest that those who return to corruption were never truly changed. God's desire that none perish (3:9) is read differently in relation to His choosing. And in 3:10 the oldest manuscripts read that the earth and its works will be ‘found’ — laid bare — rather than ‘burned up’."},{"title":"Word Families in 2 Peter","body":"2 Peter's key contrast is within the know family: ἐπίγνωσις, the full personal knowledge of Christ that saves (1:2–3, 8; 2:20), and γνῶσις, knowledge that grows (1:5–6; 3:18). Other contrasts include τηρέω and φυλάσσω (the wicked kept for judgement, Noah guarded for life, 2:4–5), ἰσχύς and δύναμις (2:11), γῆ and κόσμος (3:5–6), ῥῆμα and λόγος (3:2, 5), καινός, new in kind, for the new heavens and earth (3:13), and μακροθυμία, God's long temper (3:9, 15). New members joined many families, from ἐπόπτης (eyewitness) in the see family to ἔτος (years) in the time family."}],"roadAhead":{"intro":"The Road Ahead: 2 Peter ended with a warning against error and a call to grow in the knowledge of Christ while waiting for His coming. The next two books are 1 John, a letter about fellowship with God, walking in the light, love for one another, and testing the spirits; and 2 John, a short letter to ‘the chosen lady’ urging believers to walk in truth and love and not to welcome deceivers.","books":[{"book":"1 John","connection":"Like Peter, John writes as an eyewitness: “That which was from the beginning, that which we have heard, that which we have seen with our eyes, that which we saw, and our hands touched, concerning the Word of life” (1 John 1:1). Peter warned of false teachers; John says, “Beloved, don’t believe every spirit, but test the spirits, whether they are of God, because many false prophets have gone out into the world” (1 John 4:1). Peter called believers to holiness while they wait; John says, “Now, little children, remain in him, that when he appears, we may have boldness, and not be ashamed before him at his coming” (1 John 2:28). And the knowledge Peter urged them to grow in, John unfolds as fellowship: “But if we walk in the light, as he is in the light, we have fellowship with one another, and the blood of Jesus Christ, his Son, cleanses us from all sin” (1 John 1:7). Watch for John's tests of true faith: belief in Jesus, obedience and love."},{"book":"2 John","connection":"The shortest book of the New Testament carries 2 Peter's warning into the home. John writes as “The elder, to the chosen lady and her children, whom I love in truth” (2 John 1:1), and warns, “For many deceivers have gone out into the world, those who don’t confess that Jesus Christ came in the flesh” (2 John 1:7). Like Peter, he holds truth and love together: “This is love, that we should walk according to his commandments” (2 John 1:6). And he gives practical guidance about false teachers: “If anyone comes to you, and doesn’t bring this teaching, don’t receive him into your house, and don’t welcome him” (2 John 1:10). Watch for how John guards both love and truth."}]}},"themesMastered":[{"title":"Everything for Life and Godliness, and the Call to Grow","anchorRef":"2 Peter 1:3-11","body":"God's “divine power has granted to us all things that pertain to life and godliness” (2 Peter 1:3), and through His promises believers share the divine nature. So with all diligence they add to faith moral excellence, knowledge, self-control, perseverance, godliness, brotherly affection and love. These qualities keep them fruitful, confirm their calling and election, and ensure a rich welcome into the eternal kingdom."},{"title":"Eyewitnesses and the Sure Word","anchorRef":"2 Peter 1:16-21; 3:2, 15-16","body":"The gospel rests on eyewitness testimony: “we were eyewitnesses of his majesty” (2 Peter 1:16). And the prophetic word is a lamp in a dark place until the day dawns, because “holy men of God spoke, being moved by the Holy Spirit” (2 Peter 1:21). The words of the prophets and the commands of the apostles, including Paul's letters, are Scripture — to be heeded, not twisted."},{"title":"False Teachers Exposed","anchorRef":"2 Peter 2:1-22","body":"False teachers arise within the church, bringing in destructive heresies, denying the Master who bought them, exploiting believers with greed and fabricated words, and enticing the unstable with promises of freedom while they are slaves of corruption. Their judgement is certain. And “the Lord knows how to deliver the godly out of temptation and to keep the unrighteous under punishment for the day of judgment” (2 Peter 2:9)."},{"title":"God's Patience and the Day of the Lord","anchorRef":"2 Peter 3:3-10","body":"Scoffers mock the promise of Christ's coming, deliberately forgetting creation and the flood. But with the Lord a day is as a thousand years, and His apparent slowness is patience: “not wishing that any should perish, but that all should come to repentance” (2 Peter 3:9). Yet the day of the Lord will come like a thief, and the heavens and earth will be dissolved and laid bare."},{"title":"Holy Living While We Wait","anchorRef":"2 Peter 3:11-18","body":"Since everything will be dissolved, believers live “in holy living and godliness” (2 Peter 3:11), waiting for and hastening the day of God, looking for “new heavens and a new earth, in which righteousness dwells” (2 Peter 3:13). They are diligent to be found spotless and at peace, guard themselves against error, and “grow in the grace and knowledge of our Lord and Savior Jesus Christ” (2 Peter 3:18)."}],"experientialLesson":{"intro":"How do you walk out the Book of 2 Peter practically moving forward? Here is your experiential blueprint:","steps":[{"step":"1","title":"Climb the Ladder of Growth","lesson":"The Lesson: “Yes, and for this very cause adding on your part all diligence, in your faith supply moral excellence; and in moral excellence, knowledge” (2 Peter 1:5).","practice":"The Practice: Take eight weeks to work through the eight qualities of 1:5–7, one each week. For each, read a Scripture about it, ask God to grow it in you, and choose one concrete action to practise it. At the end, thank God that His divine power has given you everything you need, and ask a trusted friend which quality they have seen grow."},{"step":"2","title":"Anchor Yourself in the Word and Test Every Teacher","lesson":"The Lesson: “We have the more sure word of prophecy; and you do well that you heed it, as to a lamp shining in a dark place” (2 Peter 1:19).","practice":"The Practice: Establish a daily habit of reading Scripture before any other media. Keep a ‘discernment card’ with four questions — Does it exalt Jesus as Lord? Does it agree with Scripture? Does it lead to holiness and love? Does the teacher's life and use of money match the message? — and use it on every new teaching you hear this month."},{"step":"3","title":"Live Today in the Light of That Day","lesson":"The Lesson: “Therefore since all these things will be destroyed like this, what kind of people ought you to be in holy living and godliness” (2 Peter 3:11).","practice":"The Practice: Begin each morning this month by asking, ‘If Jesus returned today, what kind of person would I want Him to find?’ Then do one specific thing that answers it. Pray daily for three people who do not yet know Christ, thanking God that His patience is giving them time, and look for an opportunity to share the hope of the new creation."}]},"finalDeclaration":"“Lord Jesus, my God and Saviour, I declare that Your divine power has given me everything I need for life and godliness. Through Your precious and very great promises I share in Your nature and have escaped the corruption of the world. I will be diligent to add to my faith virtue, knowledge, self-control, perseverance, godliness, brotherly affection and love, and I will not be idle or unfruitful in knowing You. I will not forget that You cleansed me from my old sins. I will make my calling and election sure, and I will receive a rich welcome into Your eternal kingdom. I do not follow clever myths; I trust the eyewitnesses who saw Your majesty and heard the Father's voice, and I trust the Scriptures spoken by holy men carried along by the Holy Spirit. Your word is my lamp until the day dawns and the morning star rises in my heart. I reject every teacher who denies You as Master, who exploits Your people, or who promises freedom while living in corruption. I declare that You know how to rescue the godly and to hold the unrighteous for judgement. I will not scoff at Your coming. Your delay is patience, not slowness, and You do not want anyone to perish. Your day will come like a thief, so I will live in holiness and godliness, waiting for and hastening Your coming, looking for new heavens and a new earth where righteousness dwells. I will be diligent to be found by You in peace, spotless and blameless. I will guard myself from error and keep my footing. And I will grow in Your grace and in knowing You, my Lord and Saviour Jesus Christ. To You be the glory, now and to the day of eternity. In the mighty name of Jesus, Amen!”"}});
